WEBVTT

1
00:01:11.042 --> 00:01:13.966
<i>MAY 9TH 1940.</i>

2
00:01:14.647 --> 00:01:18.280
<i>HITLER HAS INVADED CZECHOSLOVAKIA,
POLAND, DENMARK, AND NORWAY.</i>

3
00:01:18.281 --> 00:01:21.892
<i>3 MILLION GERMAN TROOPS ARE NOW POISED
ON THE BELGIUM BORDER, READY TO CONQUER
THE REST OF EUROPE.</i>

4
00:01:22.091 --> 00:01:25.609
<i>IN BRITAIN, PARLIAMENT HAS LOST FAITH
IN IT'S LEADER, NEVILLE CHAMBERLAIN.</i>

5
00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:32.333
<i>THE SEARCH FOR A REPLACEMENT
HAS ALREADY BEGUN.</i>

6
00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:34.351
Order!

7
00:01:37.212 --> 00:01:38.062
Order!

8
00:01:39.007 --> 00:01:39.857
Order!

9
00:01:40.339 --> 00:01:41.739
Mr. Speaker!

10
00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:43.247
Mr. Speaker!

11
00:01:43.562 --> 00:01:44.436
Order!

12
00:01:44.775 --> 00:01:45.875
Leader...

13
00:01:46.666 --> 00:01:47.516
Order!

14
00:01:47.742 --> 00:01:50.549
The Leader of the Opposition,
Clement Attlee!

15
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.120
Mr. Speaker.

16
00:01:54.146 --> 00:01:57.268
It seems that I have not
been clear enough.

17
00:01:58.826 --> 00:02:01.989
Then let me leave no doubt

18
00:02:02.786 --> 00:02:05.805
about my feelings regarding
Mr. Chamberlain’s future

19
00:02:05.905 --> 00:02:07.063
as Prime Minister.

20
00:02:07.163 --> 00:02:09.744
Will I right, honourable gentlemen?

21
00:02:09.844 --> 00:02:11.044
Be quiet!

22
00:02:11.378 --> 00:02:12.800
Be quiet!

23
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.090
Be quiet!

24
00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:20.509
Owing to his years of inactivity
and incompetence,

25
00:02:21.177 --> 00:02:25.905
we find him personally responsible.../
Incompetence!

26
00:02:26.005 --> 00:02:31.322
Personally responsible for leaving
this nation ruinously unprepared

27
00:02:31.422 --> 00:02:34.022
to face the present Nazi peril.

28
00:02:37.844 --> 00:02:38.694
Order!

29
00:02:39.889 --> 00:02:42.333
Order!/  Shame. Shame.

30
00:02:43.641 --> 00:02:45.941
We are at war, Mr. Speaker.

31
00:02:46.162 --> 00:02:47.162
At war.

32
00:02:47.708 --> 00:02:52.841
And leaving aside whether he is
fit to be a leader in peace-time.

33
00:02:53.098 --> 00:02:57.938
He has proven himself
incapable of leading us in wartime.

34
00:03:01.976 --> 00:03:02.826
Order!

35
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:04.420
Order!

36
00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:10.587
I said order!

37
00:03:10.687 --> 00:03:14.808
Therefore, in the national interest,

38
00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.696
we the Opposition,
are willing to enter into

39
00:03:19.946 --> 00:03:26.550
a Grand Coalition with the “ruling”
Conservative Party, so-called...

40
00:03:26.808 --> 00:03:28.205
Order!

41
00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:33.619
But not, and I stress, never under
the leadership of Mr. Chamberlain

42
00:03:33.719 --> 00:03:36.312
who has lost
the confidence of this House!

43
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.369
In the country’s interest man, resign!

44
00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:44.817
Step down! And let us find a new leader!

45
00:03:46.215 --> 00:03:47.365
Shame on you!

46
00:03:47.978 --> 00:03:50.077
Shame! Shame on you!

47
00:03:51.005 --> 00:03:56.961
Where is Winston?/ Ensuring his
fingerprints aren't on the murder weapon.

48
00:03:57.223 --> 00:03:59.090
Order!

49
00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:06.690
As the Opposition refuse to join
a government headed by me,

50
00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:11.091
we must now select my successor.
Someone with

51
00:04:11.498 --> 00:04:16.043
new stands to form
a coalition government.

52
00:04:19.223 --> 00:04:20.973
I will step down tomorrow.

53
00:04:21.887 --> 00:04:22.637
But...

54
00:04:23.255 --> 00:04:30.505
I didn't want my own party, the gentlemen
I most respect, to know first.

55
00:04:31.606 --> 00:04:32.616
Yes.

56
00:04:35.409 --> 00:04:37.568
It must be Halifax./  Yeah, yeah.

57
00:04:37.668 --> 00:04:39.118
There’s no question!

58
00:04:39.356 --> 00:04:40.897
Our Foreign Secretary.

59
00:04:40.997 --> 00:04:42.947
Halifax./  Yeah, of course.

60
00:04:44.458 --> 00:04:46.308
No contest! Halifax.

61
00:04:46.694 --> 00:04:50.673
Thank you, gentlemen, I appreciate
your confidence however my time

62
00:04:50.773 --> 00:04:52.323
has not yet come.

63
00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:55.443
Then who?

64
00:04:55.957 --> 00:04:58.396
About on whomever the task may fall,

65
00:04:58.496 --> 00:05:01.593
he shall be required
to explore every avenues.

66
00:05:01.693 --> 00:05:04.412
Including better Diplomatic talks.

67
00:05:04.512 --> 00:05:05.412
Indeed.

68
00:05:05.683 --> 00:05:08.860
Towards the restoration
of peace in Europe.

69
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.610
Absolutely./  Yeah, yeah.

70
00:05:12.298 --> 00:05:14.410
Well, gentlemen, there is
only one candidate,

71
00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:17.610
only one man that
the Opposition will accept.

72
00:05:18.171 --> 00:05:19.021
Oh, no.

73
00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:20.886
Oh, surely not./  Completely no.

74
00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:21.986
No, no, no.

75
00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.466
Not now, this is totally absurdity.

76
00:05:35.071 --> 00:05:37.421
And if he stretches out his hand
and says, “Gimme”,

77
00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:42.767
you need to anticipate what he wants,
black pen, red pen, paper, or “Clop”,

78
00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:44.549
that’s his hole punch.

79
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.300
<i>"CHAMBERLAIN TO RESIGN”
CHURCHILL EXPECTED TO BE NEW PREMIER</i>

80
00:05:45.202 --> 00:05:47.945
How selfish to resign, time like this.

81
00:05:48.045 --> 00:05:50.145
D’you think they’ll take me
to Downing Street? To get the job?

82
00:05:50.245 --> 00:05:53.667
Not after that Spotted Dick
you served last week, eh?

83
00:05:54.181 --> 00:05:57.084
He mumbles, so it’s almost
impossible to catch everything.

84
00:05:57.184 --> 00:06:00.371
And be prepared to type fast,
short bursts and double-spaced,

85
00:06:00.471 --> 00:06:02.771
he hates single-spaced, hates it!

86
00:06:03.827 --> 00:06:05.027
Good luck.

87
00:06:26.487 --> 00:06:28.672
To the French Ambassador.

88
00:06:32.371 --> 00:06:34.363
With German forces,

89
00:06:35.778 --> 00:06:38.133
crossing into Holland,

90
00:06:38.233 --> 00:06:39.788
Holland alone...

91
00:06:40.576 --> 00:06:42.026
Come on, telegram!

92
00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:43.840
Holland alone,

93
00:06:45.362 --> 00:06:47.362
request reassurance,

94
00:06:48.686 --> 00:06:51.312
that French forces will now move,

95
00:06:51.932 --> 00:06:53.705
move at once,

96
00:06:53.805 --> 00:06:54.978
to...

97
00:06:55.764 --> 00:06:57.299
To protect Belgium.

98
00:06:57.399 --> 00:06:58.149
Stop.

99
00:07:02.799 --> 00:07:04.949
French ambassador, sir.

100
00:07:08.061 --> 00:07:09.661
Monsieur Ambassador!

101
00:07:16.115 --> 00:07:18.442
Ah. They’ve already invaded Belgium.

102
00:07:20.029 --> 00:07:21.986
Holland and Belgium.

103
00:07:24.239 --> 00:07:27.689
I will convey your plea to
the Prime Minister at once.

104
00:07:28.591 --> 00:07:29.491
Yes...

105
00:07:30.013 --> 00:07:32.927
The situation is still very confused.

106
00:07:36.332 --> 00:07:37.032
Yes.

107
00:07:38.416 --> 00:07:39.516
Goodbye.

108
00:07:42.198 --> 00:07:43.620
"To the French ambassador."

109
00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:45.297
"With German..."/  Scrap that.

110
00:07:45.397 --> 00:07:46.747
New telegram.

111
00:07:47.389 --> 00:07:48.789
To General Ismay...

112
00:07:49.399 --> 00:07:50.249
In the...

113
00:07:50.707 --> 00:07:52.957
What is it now?!/  Your son.

114
00:07:54.738 --> 00:07:56.338
Randolph, quickly.

115
00:07:58.758 --> 00:08:02.380
No, last night they said I might be
made Prime Minister today.

116
00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:04.193
But that was yeterday.

117
00:08:04.662 --> 00:08:06.412
Let's see what happens today.

118
00:08:07.314 --> 00:08:08.564
Let's see what...

119
00:08:09.038 --> 00:08:11.387
Let's see what Neville does today.

120
00:08:15.821 --> 00:08:16.521
Yes.

121
00:08:17.081 --> 00:08:18.551
Thank you, my boy.

122
00:08:18.651 --> 00:08:19.351
Yes.

123
00:08:20.768 --> 00:08:22.268
Yes, keep buggering on!

124
00:08:26.647 --> 00:08:28.098
Now...

125
00:08:28.491 --> 00:08:31.141
To General Ismay./  Ah, General Ismay.

126
00:08:31.609 --> 00:08:34.437
Sawyers, get rid of this, will you?

127
00:08:40.476 --> 00:08:41.876
To General Ismay.

128
00:08:42.926 --> 00:08:45.970
In the light of today's events,

129
00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:48.875
the time is

130
00:08:49.405 --> 00:08:50.555
ripe

131
00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:54.911
for many preparations

132
00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.260
to be...

133
00:08:56.756 --> 00:08:57.606
Are you...

134
00:08:58.736 --> 00:09:00.812
Striking those keys in a normal fashion?

135
00:09:00.912 --> 00:09:03.744
It’s awfully loud,
I can’t hear myself think!

136
00:09:03.844 --> 00:09:04.744
Read it back.

137
00:09:06.543 --> 00:09:07.943
"To General Ismay."

138
00:09:08.267 --> 00:09:10.843
"In the light of today's events,
the time is right..."

139
00:09:10.943 --> 00:09:13.454
Ripe! Not "right"!

140
00:09:13.554 --> 00:09:15.493
God's teeth girl!

141
00:09:15.593 --> 00:09:17.167
I said ripe!

142
00:09:17.267 --> 00:09:19.129
Ripe - ripe-ripe-ripe!

143
00:09:19.229 --> 00:09:20.479
The last sentence...

144
00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:22.819
"The-the time is ripe."

145
00:09:23.067 --> 00:09:24.453
For.../  "For..."

146
00:09:24.553 --> 00:09:26.335
For.../  Many... many... many...

147
00:09:26.435 --> 00:09:28.116
Many, many, many, many...

148
00:09:28.216 --> 00:09:31.044
How many “many”s did you
write in your compound?!

149
00:09:31.272 --> 00:09:33.146
One "many"! For...

150
00:09:33.246 --> 00:09:35.881
For many preparations.../
"For many preparations...

151
00:09:35.981 --> 00:09:37.981
...to be.../  ...to be made."

152
00:09:39.235 --> 00:09:41.670
Single-spaced! Single-spaced!

153
00:09:42.196 --> 00:09:45.546
Were you not briefed, young lady?
Single-spaced?

154
00:09:45.656 --> 00:09:47.191
Someone set it on single-spaced.
And...

155
00:09:47.291 --> 00:09:49.170
Then why did you persist?!

156
00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:50.020
I...

157
00:09:51.402 --> 00:09:54.385
Tell Evans to send me someone
who can get it right

158
00:09:54.485 --> 00:09:56.632
the first time! Go, out! Out!

159
00:09:57.509 --> 00:09:58.809
Ripe!

160
00:09:59.459 --> 00:10:00.709
T-T-T!

161
00:10:06.432 --> 00:10:07.760
He shouted at you.

162
00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.020
Did he shout at you?

163
00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.836
No./  He can be an awful brute.

164
00:10:10.936 --> 00:10:12.432
I-I made too many mistakes.

165
00:10:12.532 --> 00:10:13.919
I think you were nervous.
And he has a knack

166
00:10:13.921 --> 00:10:16.078
for drawing out the very worst in
those trying to help him the most.

167
00:10:16.177 --> 00:10:18.450
Oh it’s not him. It’s me.
He’s... he’s...

168
00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:19.600
He's a man.

169
00:10:19.765 --> 00:10:21.315
Like any other.

170
00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:23.550
Come on...

171
00:10:25.406 --> 00:10:26.156
Dear?

172
00:10:26.295 --> 00:10:28.695
The War Cabinet has been called.

173
00:10:29.057 --> 00:10:30.877
The bloody cat is under the bed again.

174
00:10:30.977 --> 00:10:32.027
My darling...

175
00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:35.170
May I tell you something that
I feel you really ought to know?

176
00:10:38.324 --> 00:10:42.059
I’ve noticed a recent deterioration
in your manner.

177
00:10:42.159 --> 00:10:44.459
You’re not so kind as you used to be.

178
00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.135
You became rough, sarcastic,
over-bearing and rude.

179
00:10:49.235 --> 00:10:50.935
Is this about the new girl?

180
00:10:52.243 --> 00:10:54.168
If the King asks us
to become Prime Minister...

181
00:10:54.169 --> 00:10:58.166
We don’t know that.../
I don’t want you to be disliked.

182
00:10:58.266 --> 00:11:00.366
More than I already am?

183
00:11:01.244 --> 00:11:03.697
My darling, you're on the brink,

184
00:11:04.026 --> 00:11:06.720
of having tremendous power,
surpassed only by the King.

185
00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:10.418
With such power you really
must try to be more kind.

186
00:11:11.684 --> 00:11:13.534
And if possible, calm.

187
00:11:17.457 --> 00:11:21.240
I want others to love
and respect you, as I do.

188
00:11:30.599 --> 00:11:32.899
Telegram for Mr. Churchill.

189
00:11:59.041 --> 00:12:01.491
<i>This is the BBC Home Service.</i>

190
00:12:02.347 --> 00:12:04.190
<i>Here is a short news bulletin.</i>

191
00:12:05.894 --> 00:12:09.844
<i>The German army invaded Holland
and Belgium early this morning.</i>

192
00:12:10.008 --> 00:12:13.308
<i>By land and land parachutes...</i>/
There's a telegram.

193
00:12:14.206 --> 00:12:17.106
<i>The Armies of the Low Countries
are resisting.</i>

194
00:12:18.118 --> 00:12:21.568
<i>An appeal for help has been made
to the Allied governments.</i>

195
00:12:21.998 --> 00:12:23.527
<i>And Brussels says...</i>/
It’s from the Palace.

196
00:12:23.627 --> 00:12:27.177
<i>...the Allied troops are
moving to their support.</i>

197
00:12:28.093 --> 00:12:31.603
<i>100 warplanes were seen over Brussels,</i>

198
00:12:31.703 --> 00:12:35.931
<i>and it's now reported that
in the first raid over Brussels,</i>

199
00:12:36.031 --> 00:12:39.695
<i>several hundred people
were killed and injured.</i>

200
00:12:40.021 --> 00:12:42.521
<i>And several buildings
have been destroyed.</i>

201
00:12:50.605 --> 00:12:51.455
Thank you.

202
00:12:52.983 --> 00:12:54.133
Miss...?

203
00:12:54.392 --> 00:12:55.242
Layton.

204
00:12:58.408 --> 00:13:00.358
You're shaking./  So are you!

205
00:13:00.876 --> 00:13:03.076
You from excitement, I from terror.

206
00:13:04.318 --> 00:13:06.174
You've been wanting this
your entire adult life.

207
00:13:06.274 --> 00:13:08.365
No, since the nursery.

208
00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:09.630
But do the public want me?

209
00:13:09.632 --> 00:13:12.241
It’s your own Party to whom
you’ll have to prove yourself.

210
00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:14.963
I’m getting the job only
because the ship is sinking.

211
00:13:15.063 --> 00:13:17.433
It's not a gift, it's a revenge.

212
00:13:17.965 --> 00:13:20.314
Let them see your true qualities.
Your courage...

213
00:13:20.414 --> 00:13:22.561
My poor judgment./  Your lack of vanity.

214
00:13:22.661 --> 00:13:24.631
My iron will./  Your sense of humor.

215
00:13:24.731 --> 00:13:25.781
Ho Ho Ho!

216
00:13:28.757 --> 00:13:30.214
Now go!/  Go?

217
00:13:30.314 --> 00:13:31.864
Be.../  Be what?

218
00:13:32.764 --> 00:13:33.764
Be yourself.

219
00:13:36.588 --> 00:13:37.588
Myself?

220
00:13:41.582 --> 00:13:46.003
Which self should I be today?

221
00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.247
One should have had power
when a young man,

222
00:13:50.347 --> 00:13:53.690
when wits were sharp, sinews strong.

223
00:13:54.177 --> 00:13:56.517
Oh, well. Lead on, MacDuff.

224
00:13:57.638 --> 00:14:03.148
When youth departs,
may wisdom prove enough.

225
00:14:54.133 --> 00:14:56.783
Hardly seems like
there’s a war on at all.

226
00:14:59.132 --> 00:15:01.732
You know I've never ridden a bus?

227
00:15:02.563 --> 00:15:03.463
Sir?

228
00:15:04.097 --> 00:15:06.947
I've never queued for bread.

229
00:15:07.789 --> 00:15:12.567
I believe I can boil an egg
but only because I've seen it done.

230
00:15:14.661 --> 00:15:17.222
The only time I tried to
ride the Underground,

231
00:15:17.322 --> 00:15:19.330
was during the General Strike.

232
00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:22.666
Clemmie dropped me
at South Kensington station.

233
00:15:22.766 --> 00:15:27.401
I went down but I got lost,
and came straight back up.

234
00:15:28.082 --> 00:15:29.232
It was awful.

235
00:15:31.174 --> 00:15:33.648
Why not Halifax?
I favour Halifax.

236
00:15:33.748 --> 00:15:35.148
I wanted Halifax.

237
00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.220
The Lords wanted Halifax.

238
00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.393
Perhaps Halifax wanted Halifax.

239
00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:44.903
Then why have I been forced
to send for Churchill?

240
00:15:45.152 --> 00:15:47.240
Because he's the only
member of our party,

241
00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.790
who has the support of the Opposition.

242
00:15:49.933 --> 00:15:52.828
His record is a litany of catastrophe.

243
00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.795
Gallipoli, 25,000 dead.

244
00:15:55.895 --> 00:15:58.800
The India Policy. Russian Civil War.

245
00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:00.250
The Gold Standard.

246
00:16:01.837 --> 00:16:04.428
The-the-the Abdication.
And now this...

247
00:16:04.528 --> 00:16:07.307
Norway ‘adventure’.
What, eighteen hundred men?

248
00:16:07.407 --> 00:16:12.300
One aircraft carrier, two cruisers,
seven destroyers and a submarine.

249
00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.389
Winston lacks judgement.

250
00:16:14.611 --> 00:16:19.250
He was right about Hitler./ But even
a stopped clock is right twice a day.

251
00:16:23.113 --> 00:16:25.843
First Lord of the Admiralty,
Mr. Winston Churchill!

252
00:16:25.943 --> 00:16:26.967
Early.

253
00:16:27.067 --> 00:16:28.117
Keen.

254
00:16:32.386 --> 00:16:37.315
I accept your resignation,
but I want you to know, how

255
00:16:38.331 --> 00:16:41.081
cruelly I think you’ve been treated.

256
00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:43.650
Thank you, Your Majesty.

257
00:16:47.887 --> 00:16:49.587
This way, my Lord.

258
00:17:27.711 --> 00:17:30.111
Your Majesty./  Mr. Churchill.

259
00:17:31.552 --> 00:17:37.338
I believe you know
why I have asked you here today.

260
00:17:37.438 --> 00:17:39.938
Sir, I simply cannot imagine why.

261
00:17:44.788 --> 00:17:49.822
It is my duty to invite you to take up
the position of Prime Minister

262
00:17:49.922 --> 00:17:51.634
of this United Kingdom.

263
00:17:52.716 --> 00:17:54.566
Will you form a Government?

264
00:17:55.689 --> 00:17:56.689
I will.

265
00:17:57.407 --> 00:17:58.457
Very well.

266
00:18:13.809 --> 00:18:15.759
That was... quite easy.

267
00:18:16.675 --> 00:18:17.825
Yes, it was.

268
00:18:22.954 --> 00:18:26.448
I believe we are to meet regularly.

269
00:18:27.604 --> 00:18:30.254
Once a week, I'm afraid.
How was...

270
00:18:32.225 --> 00:18:33.825
How if Monday?

271
00:18:34.774 --> 00:18:38.889
I shall endeavour to be available,
on Mondays./ Four o’clock?

272
00:18:38.989 --> 00:18:40.839
I nap at 4.

273
00:18:42.896 --> 00:18:44.666
Is that... permissible?

274
00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:47.123
No. But necessary.

275
00:18:47.337 --> 00:18:48.640
I work late.

276
00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:50.540
Then perhaps lunchtime?

277
00:18:50.916 --> 00:18:51.866
Lunch?

278
00:18:53.015 --> 00:18:54.065
Mondays.

279
00:18:57.766 --> 00:18:59.618
Your Majesty.

280
00:18:59.847 --> 00:19:01.097
Prime Minister.

281
00:19:31.261 --> 00:19:32.374
Greetings, gentlemen.

282
00:19:32.474 --> 00:19:34.361
Mr. Churchill, welcome to number 10.

283
00:19:34.461 --> 00:19:35.413
Thank you, sir.

284
00:19:35.513 --> 00:19:37.319
Mr. Churchill, what's your agenda?

285
00:19:37.419 --> 00:19:38.569
Agenda?

286
00:19:39.022 --> 00:19:40.822
A glass of Pol Roger.

287
00:19:41.892 --> 00:19:44.464
Come here, Sir!/
You have your pictures.

288
00:19:44.564 --> 00:19:45.623
Prime Minister.

289
00:19:45.723 --> 00:19:46.623
Anthony.

290
00:19:47.049 --> 00:19:48.499
How was the King?

291
00:19:49.035 --> 00:19:49.935
Anxious.

292
00:19:50.975 --> 00:19:53.805
He's never forgive me for supporting his

293
00:19:54.371 --> 00:19:57.371
brother's marriage
to Wallis Simpson.

294
00:19:58.076 --> 00:19:59.506
You only have to meet him once a week.

295
00:19:59.507 --> 00:20:02.575
That’s like saying you only
have to have your tooth pulled

296
00:20:02.675 --> 00:20:04.275
once a week.

297
00:20:04.838 --> 00:20:07.438
Composition of your war cabinet?

298
00:20:07.644 --> 00:20:09.094
Who should sit on it?

299
00:20:10.212 --> 00:20:12.012
Chamberlain, of course.

300
00:20:13.673 --> 00:20:15.740
And the Reverend “Holy Fox”.

301
00:20:16.515 --> 00:20:17.415
Halifax.

302
00:20:18.851 --> 00:20:20.357
Keeping your enemies close?

303
00:20:20.457 --> 00:20:21.757
Oh, more than that.

304
00:20:22.342 --> 00:20:25.242
Without them, the party
will get rid of me.

305
00:20:25.398 --> 00:20:28.289
Oh! And that sheep
in sheep’s clothing, Attlee.

306
00:20:29.198 --> 00:20:31.286
Come on Daddy, everyone’s waiting.

307
00:20:31.386 --> 00:20:33.952
I'll be there in a jiffy, my love.

308
00:20:35.394 --> 00:20:38.926
I heard that before you were asked,
they offered it to Lord Halifax.

309
00:20:39.026 --> 00:20:40.226
No, I doubt that.

310
00:20:40.703 --> 00:20:42.953
Halifax would never turn it down.

311
00:20:43.688 --> 00:20:45.838
He's the fourth son of an Earl.

312
00:20:46.315 --> 00:20:48.865
Fourth sons turn nothing down.

313
00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:53.460
I only wish the position had come
your way in better times, sir.

314
00:20:54.284 --> 00:20:56.184
You have an enormous task ahead of you.

315
00:20:56.329 --> 00:20:58.679
My only hope it's not too late.

316
00:20:58.998 --> 00:21:01.098
I am very much afraid it is.

317
00:21:02.851 --> 00:21:04.900
But we can only do our best.

318
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:06.811
Hip hip.../  Hurray!

319
00:21:06.911 --> 00:21:08.696
Hip hip./  Hurray!!

320
00:21:08.796 --> 00:21:11.314
Good heavens, what a frightful sight!

321
00:21:11.414 --> 00:21:13.514
Oh, a toast!/  Yes, a speech!

322
00:21:14.137 --> 00:21:15.787
Yes./  The bottle.

323
00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:18.080
Come here./  Yes.

324
00:21:19.358 --> 00:21:20.758
My darling husband...

325
00:21:22.373 --> 00:21:23.573
Some of you might not know of it.

326
00:21:23.574 --> 00:21:26.310
On the eve of our marriage
I got “cold feet”,

327
00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.896
but as I’d already called off
two engagements

328
00:21:28.996 --> 00:21:32.844
by the age of 21, I was in danger of
gaining a reputation for being a ‘bolter’,

329
00:21:32.944 --> 00:21:35.157
and it would’ve been
poor form to call off a third!

330
00:21:35.257 --> 00:21:37.066
Hear hear./  Lucky for Daddy.

331
00:21:37.166 --> 00:21:40.466
But the real reason for my
wintry feet was because I knew,

332
00:21:41.228 --> 00:21:42.428
even then,

333
00:21:42.685 --> 00:21:45.935
that his priority would be public life.

334
00:21:46.363 --> 00:21:48.475
It worried a young girl greatly
this wretched thought of

335
00:21:48.575 --> 00:21:50.469
eternally coming second.

336
00:21:51.269 --> 00:21:53.069
But so it has proven to be.

337
00:21:53.481 --> 00:21:56.366
And in due course our children
would have to make their peace

338
00:21:56.466 --> 00:21:57.916
with this same fact.

339
00:22:00.747 --> 00:22:02.497
We all did, you see.

340
00:22:02.822 --> 00:22:04.022
In our own ways.

341
00:22:07.198 --> 00:22:12.017
And now, today,
we all received our reward,

342
00:22:12.847 --> 00:22:17.936
proof that our small sacrifice
was for a far, far greater good.

343
00:22:19.138 --> 00:22:23.809
I give you, your father,
my beloved husband,

344
00:22:24.727 --> 00:22:26.127
the Prime Minister.

345
00:22:26.897 --> 00:22:28.297
Prime Minister!

346
00:22:28.824 --> 00:22:30.046
Here’s to...

347
00:22:33.093 --> 00:22:35.508
To not buggering it up.

348
00:22:35.608 --> 00:22:37.758
"To not buggering it up!"

349
00:22:38.902 --> 00:22:40.889
Belgium was a ploy.

350
00:22:40.989 --> 00:22:44.058
They punched through
the Ardennes into France

351
00:22:44.158 --> 00:22:46.811
and crossed the Meuse in under 24 hours!

352
00:22:46.911 --> 00:22:48.933
No-one can cross
the Meuse River in 24 hours.

353
00:22:49.033 --> 00:22:51.050
Apparently the Germans can.

354
00:23:25.366 --> 00:23:27.355
Look to Chamberlain's handkerchief.

355
00:23:27.455 --> 00:23:29.611
If he waves it at the end
of Churchill’s speech,

356
00:23:29.711 --> 00:23:31.361
we show approval.

357
00:23:32.527 --> 00:23:34.577
If not, keep quiet.

358
00:23:36.083 --> 00:23:37.890
The Prime Minister!

359
00:23:48.654 --> 00:23:49.641
Here we go.

360
00:23:52.983 --> 00:23:54.383
Mr. Speaker.

361
00:23:55.451 --> 00:24:01.789
On Friday evening last,
I received His Majesty’s commission

362
00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:04.700
to form a new administration.

363
00:24:06.314 --> 00:24:11.969
It was the evident wish and will
of Parliament, and the Nation,

364
00:24:12.583 --> 00:24:17.010
that this should be conceived
on the broadest possible basis,

365
00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:20.174
and that it should include all parties.

366
00:24:20.274 --> 00:24:21.514
<i>Yes.</i>

367
00:24:23.385 --> 00:24:26.135
A War Cabinet has been convened...

368
00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:28.450
No...

369
00:24:29.398 --> 00:24:30.391
Correction.

370
00:24:30.491 --> 00:24:31.941
Has been formed.

371
00:24:34.065 --> 00:24:35.065
"Formed."

372
00:24:35.255 --> 00:24:39.600
A War Cabinet has been
formed of five members,

373
00:24:40.026 --> 00:24:42.926
representing with
the Opposition parties,

374
00:24:43.439 --> 00:24:45.205
and the unity of the Nation.

375
00:24:47.785 --> 00:24:48.585
Read.

376
00:24:49.308 --> 00:24:51.521
<i>The three party Leaders
have agreed to serve,</i>

377
00:24:51.621 --> 00:24:52.959
<i>either in the War Cabinet</i>

378
00:24:53.059 --> 00:24:55.666
Or in high executive office./
...<i>or in high executive office.</i>

379
00:24:55.766 --> 00:24:58.010
With this agreement's in placed,

380
00:24:58.733 --> 00:25:04.133
I now invite the House by the
resolution which stands in my name,

381
00:25:04.711 --> 00:25:12.686
to record it's approval, and to declare
it's confidence in the new government.

382
00:25:16.152 --> 00:25:17.152
Miss?

383
00:25:18.107 --> 00:25:20.800
I’m coming out in a state of nature!

384
00:25:27.425 --> 00:25:28.975
It must be remembered

385
00:25:29.827 --> 00:25:32.897
that we are in the preliminary stage

386
00:25:32.997 --> 00:25:36.090
of one of the greatest
battles in history

387
00:25:37.215 --> 00:25:41.267
and that many preparations
have to be made here at home.

388
00:25:42.856 --> 00:25:45.432
Sir, I take up my task

389
00:25:45.532 --> 00:25:50.850
with buoyancy and hope
and say to the House,

390
00:25:51.193 --> 00:25:54.571
as I said to those who have
joined the government.

391
00:25:54.671 --> 00:26:02.228
I have nothing to offer,
but blood, toil, tears and sweat.

392
00:26:05.261 --> 00:26:08.772
We have before us an ordeal
of the most grievous kind.

393
00:26:10.018 --> 00:26:14.251
We have before us many,
many long months of struggle

394
00:26:14.513 --> 00:26:15.663
and of suffering.

395
00:26:16.667 --> 00:26:19.605
You ask, what is our policy?

396
00:26:20.996 --> 00:26:28.807
I say it is to wage war by sea,
land and air, with all our might

397
00:26:29.268 --> 00:26:33.532
and with all the strength God
can give us,

398
00:26:34.543 --> 00:26:39.430
to wage war against a monstrous tyranny

399
00:26:40.319 --> 00:26:45.773
never surpassed in the dark and
lamentable catalogue of human crime.

400
00:26:45.873 --> 00:26:48.567
That is our policy.

401
00:26:49.375 --> 00:26:52.150
If you ask, what is our aim?

402
00:26:52.919 --> 00:26:55.699
I can answer in one word.

403
00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:58.210
Victory.

404
00:26:58.990 --> 00:27:04.951
Victory at all costs.
Victory, in spite of all terror.

405
00:27:05.628 --> 00:27:10.213
Victory however long and
hard the road may be.

406
00:27:10.701 --> 00:27:16.770
For without victory,
there can be no survival.

407
00:27:43.358 --> 00:27:44.358
Contempt.

408
00:27:45.437 --> 00:27:48.251
Well, you have to start somewhere./
Well, you have to start somewhere.

409
00:27:48.252 --> 00:27:49.002
Snap.

410
00:27:57.921 --> 00:28:00.752
Our policy is to wage war.

411
00:28:01.482 --> 00:28:02.866
At all costs.

412
00:28:03.066 --> 00:28:04.672
No survival.

413
00:28:04.772 --> 00:28:08.868
Good God, he is incapable of even
pronouncing the word peace,

414
00:28:08.968 --> 00:28:11.168
let alone entering into negotiations.

415
00:28:12.399 --> 00:28:14.244
Awful, the thought
that I shall never see

416
00:28:14.245 --> 00:28:16.394
my country at peace again.

417
00:28:20.189 --> 00:28:21.539
I have cancer.

418
00:28:26.459 --> 00:28:27.659
Oh, Neville.

419
00:28:33.375 --> 00:28:35.975
Winston must be removed from office.

420
00:28:36.226 --> 00:28:39.029
If we can get him to declare
that he refuses to even consider

421
00:28:39.129 --> 00:28:40.787
peace negotiations with Germany,

422
00:28:40.887 --> 00:28:43.887
then you and I will perhaps
have clear grounds to resign.

423
00:28:44.927 --> 00:28:47.530
that will force a vote
of “no confidence”.

424
00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:49.113
The party couldn't count for that,

425
00:28:49.213 --> 00:28:51.463
you’re the chairman for heavens sake.

426
00:28:52.091 --> 00:28:53.258
He’ll be finished.

427
00:28:53.358 --> 00:28:56.208
And would you agree
to be Prime Minister?

428
00:28:57.080 --> 00:28:57.830
Well...

429
00:28:59.553 --> 00:29:01.253
With Winston out of the way who can say.

430
00:29:01.254 --> 00:29:03.694
But the important thing, Neville,

431
00:29:03.754 --> 00:29:07.204
is that your policies
would be back on the table.

432
00:29:07.497 --> 00:29:10.047
Peace and the protection of our nation.

433
00:29:10.367 --> 00:29:11.367
On record.

434
00:29:12.147 --> 00:29:14.198
I beg a pardon?/
He must declare on record

435
00:29:14.298 --> 00:29:17.358
his refusal to engage in peace talks.

436
00:29:18.425 --> 00:29:20.125
We must have it in writing.

437
00:29:55.233 --> 00:29:56.566
<i>WAR OFFICE, LONDON</i>

438
00:29:56.666 --> 00:29:58.053
<i>CHURCHILL WAR ROOM - PASS</i>

439
00:30:00.068 --> 00:30:00.918
Thank you.

440
00:30:03.006 --> 00:30:04.690
Miss Layton./  Good morning.

441
00:30:04.790 --> 00:30:05.740
Follow me.

442
00:30:19.895 --> 00:30:22.045
Good morning./  Thank you, sir.

443
00:30:23.855 --> 00:30:25.155
Thank you, madam.

444
00:30:27.619 --> 00:30:29.013
What goes on down here?

445
00:30:29.113 --> 00:30:31.763
That’s a need to know. And you don’t.

446
00:30:32.111 --> 00:30:34.711
The lavatory?/  For the PM’s use only.

447
00:30:35.393 --> 00:30:38.243
Sleeping quarters, for
when you miss the last train.

448
00:30:40.557 --> 00:30:41.357
Come.

449
00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:45.360
That's the map room.

450
00:30:46.090 --> 00:30:48.530
No women allowed./
What department is this?

451
00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:50.668
Indiscretion in conversation,
or any other form,

452
00:30:50.768 --> 00:30:53.095
within or without these rooms
regarding what happens here

453
00:30:53.096 --> 00:30:55.838
is a statutory offense and
punishable by up to two years

454
00:30:55.938 --> 00:30:57.988
imprisonment with hard labour.

455
00:30:58.714 --> 00:31:00.614
Clear?/  Crystal.

456
00:31:01.331 --> 00:31:02.081
Good.

457
00:31:03.702 --> 00:31:06.802
That's the War Cabinet room.
Never...

458
00:31:08.671 --> 00:31:11.071
I Don’t mean to be rough on you but...

459
00:31:12.131 --> 00:31:13.381
Them's the rules.

460
00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:17.306
This is the typists room.

461
00:31:17.406 --> 00:31:19.606
Good morning, sir./  Ladies.

462
00:31:21.449 --> 00:31:22.633
And here’s you.

463
00:31:30.739 --> 00:31:33.417
Belgium and Holland
may fall in any hour.

464
00:31:33.517 --> 00:31:34.510
The French?

465
00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:37.330
The entire French ninth army,
some two hundred thousand men

466
00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:38.580
have capitulated.

467
00:31:39.025 --> 00:31:39.825
All of them?!

468
00:31:40.560 --> 00:31:42.192
Capitulated. Deserted.

469
00:31:42.292 --> 00:31:43.442
It was a rout.

470
00:31:43.841 --> 00:31:45.492
All our land forces, roughly

471
00:31:45.592 --> 00:31:48.166
300,000 men, are now in full retreat.

472
00:31:48.266 --> 00:31:50.410
Air-cover? For our troops?

473
00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:52.317
The Luftwaffe control the skies.

474
00:31:52.417 --> 00:31:55.121
We simply don’t have
enough planes to challenge them.

475
00:31:55.221 --> 00:31:58.371
In fact, I strongly recommend we stop
sending our precious fighter-planes

476
00:31:58.471 --> 00:32:00.221
to be wasted in France.

477
00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:02.370
Save them for our own defence.

478
00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:07.155
And our navy sits idle,
neutralized, useless.

479
00:32:07.539 --> 00:32:09.862
Our fleets no sooner
come within their range,

480
00:32:09.962 --> 00:32:13.062
than we come under
blistering air attack.

481
00:32:13.673 --> 00:32:15.822
Their speed is devastating.

482
00:32:16.634 --> 00:32:19.451
Panzer tanks. Plunging rapidly
westward, through the centre.

483
00:32:19.551 --> 00:32:23.436
But they will have to
pause for fuel supplies.

484
00:32:23.536 --> 00:32:25.251
This is not the last war, Sir.

485
00:32:25.351 --> 00:32:28.851
Their tanks can stop for fuel
at a petrol station.

486
00:32:29.524 --> 00:32:30.940
Petrol station?

487
00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:33.340
The road to Paris now lies open.

488
00:32:33.796 --> 00:32:36.496
7 million refugees are on the move.

489
00:32:37.791 --> 00:32:41.141
Collectively we are looking at the collapse
of Western Europe in a few days.

490
00:32:42.183 --> 00:32:44.183
Should the public be told?

491
00:32:50.529 --> 00:32:51.579
Not yet.

492
00:32:52.815 --> 00:32:57.452
First, we must rouse our old friends
to an heroic resistance.

493
00:32:58.904 --> 00:33:01.702
France must be saved.

494
00:34:07.231 --> 00:34:08.481
Premiere.
[ Prime Minister ]

495
00:34:10.346 --> 00:34:13.693
"Although our situation...

496
00:34:15.086 --> 00:34:16.874
...is very serious."

497
00:34:19.842 --> 00:34:22.192
"It's not the first time...

498
00:34:23.470 --> 00:34:24.470
...that we are...

499
00:34:26.315 --> 00:34:27.765
...facing...

500
00:34:28.259 --> 00:34:29.710
...a crisis...

501
00:34:30.533 --> 00:34:31.683
...together."

502
00:34:34.905 --> 00:34:37.316
Perhaps in English, Prime Minister.

503
00:34:39.720 --> 00:34:44.696
We have survived
crisis before, and I am...

504
00:34:45.078 --> 00:34:47.328
I'm confident we will survive this one.

505
00:34:48.447 --> 00:34:50.847
Tell me how you plan to counter-attack.

506
00:34:53.048 --> 00:34:54.348
There is no plan.

507
00:34:58.366 --> 00:35:00.437
You must counter-attack!

508
00:35:01.314 --> 00:35:03.258
You... you must...

509
00:35:04.119 --> 00:35:05.207
"You must...

510
00:35:07.177 --> 00:35:08.836
...fight!"

511
00:35:09.937 --> 00:35:11.137
"It must!"

512
00:35:11.893 --> 00:35:13.693
I do not believe this...

513
00:35:14.355 --> 00:35:15.305
This...

514
00:35:15.852 --> 00:35:19.266
Panzer breakthrough is a real invasion.

515
00:35:19.366 --> 00:35:21.547
Not a real invasion?/  No...

516
00:35:21.955 --> 00:35:26.028
As long as their tanks are not
supported by infantry units.

517
00:35:26.128 --> 00:35:31.581
They are merely little flags,
stuck on the map,

518
00:35:31.681 --> 00:35:35.013
because the tank crews
cannot support themselves.

519
00:35:35.113 --> 00:35:38.912
No, I refuse to see
in this spectacular raid

520
00:35:39.012 --> 00:35:43.377
of the German tanks, a real invasion!

521
00:35:46.964 --> 00:35:48.164
He's delusional.

522
00:35:48.667 --> 00:35:50.567
Completely delusional.

523
00:35:50.667 --> 00:35:51.949
He's English!

524
00:35:55.473 --> 00:35:56.623
He is an actor.

525
00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:59.123
In love with the sound of his own voice.

526
00:35:59.223 --> 00:36:00.573
Oh I love to listen to him,

527
00:36:01.517 --> 00:36:04.065
but we must never take his advice.

528
00:36:04.165 --> 00:36:06.264
Has a hundred ideas a day.

529
00:36:06.364 --> 00:36:07.514
Four of them are good,

530
00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:10.065
the other 96 downright dangerous.

531
00:36:10.165 --> 00:36:13.315
His father used to say,
a great orator, but...

532
00:36:13.683 --> 00:36:15.842
Until he lost his mind to syphilis.

533
00:36:15.942 --> 00:36:17.329
How nations suffer...

534
00:36:17.429 --> 00:36:20.245
...for the sins of their fathers./
My opinion?

535
00:36:20.345 --> 00:36:22.845
At this critical juncture
for the empire,

536
00:36:24.592 --> 00:36:26.743
we have a drunkard at the wheel.

537
00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:28.800
Wakes with a scotch,

538
00:36:29.909 --> 00:36:32.237
bottle of champagne at lunch,

539
00:36:32.337 --> 00:36:33.737
another at dinner,

540
00:36:34.634 --> 00:36:37.260
brandy and port till the wee hours...

541
00:36:38.081 --> 00:36:40.519
I wouldn’t let him borrow my bicycle!

542
00:36:40.619 --> 00:36:43.269
He’s a Conservative
 who defects to the Liberals,

543
00:36:43.417 --> 00:36:45.818
lobs grenades at us for 10 years,

544
00:36:45.918 --> 00:36:48.184
then flops Conservative again,

545
00:36:48.284 --> 00:36:49.834
as it bloody suits him!

546
00:36:49.934 --> 00:36:53.126
Sorry, but he stands for one thing,

547
00:36:53.226 --> 00:36:54.275
himself!

548
00:36:55.146 --> 00:36:57.596
We might have to replace him, Bertie.

549
00:36:59.014 --> 00:37:00.164
Replace him?

550
00:37:00.788 --> 00:37:02.738
We must strive for peace,

551
00:37:03.189 --> 00:37:05.271
so every son and daughter of this land

552
00:37:05.371 --> 00:37:09.021
could emerge from this crisis
with something recognisable as 'home.'

553
00:37:10.939 --> 00:37:13.589
Spoken like a true Prime Minister.

554
00:37:28.472 --> 00:37:30.174
"V" for victory.

555
00:37:32.174 --> 00:37:35.124
Sir, you need to reply
to the Lord Privy Seal.

556
00:37:36.844 --> 00:37:37.894
Yeah, yeah...

557
00:37:38.162 --> 00:37:40.101
Tell the Lord Privy Seal,

558
00:37:40.201 --> 00:37:42.407
I’m sealed in the Privy.

559
00:37:42.915 --> 00:37:44.916
And I can only deal with one

560
00:37:45.016 --> 00:37:46.466
shit at a time.

561
00:37:53.789 --> 00:37:56.065
The broadcast is tonight, so...

562
00:37:56.165 --> 00:37:57.870
Don’t spare me, Anthony...

563
00:37:57.970 --> 00:37:59.070
Be frank.

564
00:38:07.822 --> 00:38:08.922
I don't think so.

565
00:38:09.685 --> 00:38:11.435
You don’t think so, what?

566
00:38:11.757 --> 00:38:14.761
You’re suggesting we’re somehow winning.

567
00:38:14.861 --> 00:38:16.011
We’re not.

568
00:38:16.742 --> 00:38:17.935
No but...

569
00:38:18.639 --> 00:38:20.189
it will inspire them.

570
00:38:20.596 --> 00:38:23.114
Winston, I know.../  Anthony, Anthony.

571
00:38:23.214 --> 00:38:25.272
I am going to imbue them

572
00:38:25.561 --> 00:38:30.310
with a spirit of feeling they don’t
yet know they have!

573
00:38:30.559 --> 00:38:32.459
You asked my opinion.

574
00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:34.970
I caution against it.

575
00:38:37.205 --> 00:38:38.155
Cicero.

576
00:38:41.291 --> 00:38:42.241
Cicero!

577
00:38:44.992 --> 00:38:47.335
"...and if fortune is adverse...

578
00:38:48.669 --> 00:38:51.575
...something, something..."

579
00:38:59.630 --> 00:39:01.330
It's not here... I leave it here.

580
00:39:02.767 --> 00:39:03.667
Clemmie!

581
00:39:05.063 --> 00:39:05.963
Cat!

582
00:39:06.178 --> 00:39:07.078
Clemmie!

583
00:39:07.308 --> 00:39:10.307
We cannot pay the bills./
My copy of...

584
00:39:10.407 --> 00:39:13.019
...Cicero. Did you shelve it?/
Did you hear what I said?

585
00:39:13.119 --> 00:39:14.919
What was that?/  We're broke.

586
00:39:15.388 --> 00:39:16.138
Oh...

587
00:39:16.702 --> 00:39:17.502
Out!

588
00:39:17.708 --> 00:39:19.658
Come on! Everybody out!

589
00:39:21.295 --> 00:39:23.593
I dare not write another cheque.

590
00:39:23.693 --> 00:39:25.779
Well, I’ll economise.

591
00:39:25.879 --> 00:39:28.274
Only four cigars a day!

592
00:39:28.453 --> 00:39:30.064
You’re insufferable!

593
00:39:34.747 --> 00:39:36.147
Anything else?

594
00:39:36.899 --> 00:39:37.599
Yes.

595
00:39:38.193 --> 00:39:39.922
My love for you./  Oh!

596
00:39:40.022 --> 00:39:41.972
How much have you had
to drink this morning?

597
00:39:43.626 --> 00:39:45.095
I see you now

598
00:39:46.441 --> 00:39:48.791
as I first saw you

599
00:39:49.257 --> 00:39:51.235
in 1904.

600
00:39:51.488 --> 00:39:54.133
And I simply stood, speechless.

601
00:39:54.233 --> 00:39:57.268
I must have been very beautiful
to have achieved that miraculous effect.

602
00:39:57.368 --> 00:39:59.318
Four years till we saw each other again.

603
00:39:59.754 --> 00:40:01.015
They went by in an absolute flash.

604
00:40:01.115 --> 00:40:03.815
You didn’t lack for admirers, your...

605
00:40:03.987 --> 00:40:07.130
"Loyal attendant" in Sidney Peel.

606
00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.830
Brilliant man./  Lionel Earle.

607
00:40:09.998 --> 00:40:11.248
Wonderful dancer.

608
00:40:12.396 --> 00:40:15.096
And then at Lady St Hellier’s
dinner party,

609
00:40:15.398 --> 00:40:16.948
who should show up?

610
00:40:18.363 --> 00:40:19.463
Pig.

611
00:40:21.841 --> 00:40:23.241
Just the same.

612
00:40:34.247 --> 00:40:36.097
Are we terribly old?

613
00:40:36.754 --> 00:40:38.454
Yes. I’m afraid you are.

614
00:40:38.618 --> 00:40:40.068
Oh, you're beast!

615
00:40:40.429 --> 00:40:41.379
I'm...

616
00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:47.837
Will you hear me read my speech

617
00:40:47.937 --> 00:40:49.633
for tonight’s broadcast?

618
00:40:50.837 --> 00:40:53.545
Prime Minister! Prime Minister,
the situation in France,

619
00:40:53.645 --> 00:40:55.711
is it true we're in full retreat?

620
00:40:55.811 --> 00:40:57.411
Is France lost?

621
00:41:04.616 --> 00:41:09.016
Above the bench is the champ's goat,
under the bench is the crack's goat.

622
00:41:09.761 --> 00:41:14.021
If you will sit here, at your desk.

623
00:41:16.082 --> 00:41:18.329
And speak slowly and clearly.

624
00:41:19.733 --> 00:41:20.933
Into the microphone.

625
00:41:21.719 --> 00:41:22.569
So...

626
00:41:23.122 --> 00:41:24.272
If you're ready,

627
00:41:24.871 --> 00:41:26.121
on the stroke of 9

628
00:41:26.811 --> 00:41:30.261
the light will come on and
we shall go live to the nation.

629
00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:33.410
Nine o'clock.

630
00:41:33.954 --> 00:41:35.004
Red light.

631
00:41:36.492 --> 00:41:37.542
You begin.

632
00:41:38.043 --> 00:41:38.943
Yes?

633
00:41:40.337 --> 00:41:41.387
One moment.

634
00:41:48.453 --> 00:41:49.803
Prime Minister.

635
00:41:49.903 --> 00:41:50.853
Are we ready?

636
00:41:51.271 --> 00:41:52.021
I...

637
00:41:52.947 --> 00:41:54.897
One moment, one moment...

638
00:42:00.657 --> 00:42:03.777
We are going live./
One moment, damn you!

639
00:42:10.937 --> 00:42:11.637
And...

640
00:42:11.861 --> 00:42:12.761
...four,

641
00:42:13.386 --> 00:42:14.086
...three,

642
00:42:14.690 --> 00:42:15.390
...two,

643
00:42:15.898 --> 00:42:16.598
...one.

644
00:42:37.270 --> 00:42:42.238
I speak to you for
the first time as Prime Minister,

645
00:42:43.256 --> 00:42:47.266
in a solemn hour for
the life of our country,

646
00:42:48.323 --> 00:42:49.873
of our Empire,

647
00:42:50.461 --> 00:42:51.961
of our allies,

648
00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:53.710
and above all,

649
00:42:54.504 --> 00:42:56.404
of the cause of Freedom.

650
00:42:57.501 --> 00:43:02.534
A tremendous battle is raging
in France and Flanders.

651
00:43:04.429 --> 00:43:08.343
The Germans, by a remarkable combination

652
00:43:08.973 --> 00:43:10.573
of air bombing

653
00:43:10.885 --> 00:43:12.673
and heavily armored tanks,

654
00:43:13.584 --> 00:43:16.184
have broken through the French defenses

655
00:43:16.394 --> 00:43:18.864
north of the Maginot Line,

656
00:43:19.919 --> 00:43:22.769
<i>and strong columns
of their armoured vehicles</i>

657
00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:25.670
<i>are ravaging the open country...</i>

658
00:43:26.554 --> 00:43:28.554
<i>Which for the first day or two,</i>

659
00:43:29.042 --> 00:43:30.892
<i>was without defenders.</i>

660
00:43:31.975 --> 00:43:32.875
<i>However...</i>

661
00:43:33.867 --> 00:43:37.100
<i>I have invincible confidence</i>

662
00:43:37.567 --> 00:43:40.367
<i>in the French Army and it's leaders.</i>

663
00:43:41.157 --> 00:43:44.307
Only a very small part
of that splendid Army

664
00:43:44.626 --> 00:43:47.376
has yet been heavily engaged,

665
00:43:47.646 --> 00:43:50.419
only a very small part of France

666
00:43:50.859 --> 00:43:52.509
has yet been invaded.

667
00:43:53.157 --> 00:43:54.578
<i>Side by side,</i>

668
00:43:54.997 --> 00:43:57.692
<i>the British and French peoples</i>

669
00:43:58.055 --> 00:44:00.048
<i>have advanced...</i>/  Advanced?

670
00:44:00.148 --> 00:44:03.689
<i>...to rescue not only Europe,</i>/
How bloody dare he!

671
00:44:03.789 --> 00:44:05.489
<i>...but mankind</i>

672
00:44:05.611 --> 00:44:10.026
from the foulest and most
soul-destroying tyranny

673
00:44:11.343 --> 00:44:13.325
<i>which has ever darkened and stained</i>

674
00:44:13.425 --> 00:44:15.658
<i>the pages of history.</i>

675
00:44:16.271 --> 00:44:19.900
<i>But now one bond unites us all,</i>

676
00:44:20.303 --> 00:44:22.153
<i>to wage war</i>

677
00:44:22.568 --> 00:44:25.081
<i>until victory is won,</i>

678
00:44:25.600 --> 00:44:27.856
<i>and never to surrender ourselves</i>

679
00:44:28.490 --> 00:44:30.693
<i>to servitude and shame,</i>

680
00:44:31.859 --> 00:44:33.459
<i>whatever the cost</i>

681
00:44:34.749 --> 00:44:36.749
<i>and the agony may be...</i>

682
00:44:37.379 --> 00:44:39.634
Conquer we must,

683
00:44:40.438 --> 00:44:43.133
as conquer we shall.

684
00:44:50.027 --> 00:44:51.477
Well done, sir.

685
00:45:57.912 --> 00:46:00.612
Oh, I thought you did marvelously.

686
00:46:01.515 --> 00:46:05.433
For the last ten years I was
the only one who told them the truth.

687
00:46:06.381 --> 00:46:07.631
Until tonight.

688
00:46:09.445 --> 00:46:11.245
There's no advance.

689
00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:14.660
It's a shambles.

690
00:46:15.963 --> 00:46:17.663
We're in full retreat.

691
00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:23.421
But would you be serving them tonight
by denying them sleep, and...

692
00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:25.610
Terrifying their children?

693
00:46:25.885 --> 00:46:27.571
Even if the terror is coming?!

694
00:46:27.671 --> 00:46:29.671
Because the terror is coming.

695
00:46:31.686 --> 00:46:33.686
There is time enough for the truth.

696
00:46:43.668 --> 00:46:44.668
Have you seen this?

697
00:46:49.391 --> 00:46:50.891
What’s so funny?

698
00:46:52.253 --> 00:46:53.503
Prime Minister.

699
00:46:53.603 --> 00:46:55.553
Sir./  Yes. What is it?

700
00:46:57.293 --> 00:46:58.193
Perhaps...

701
00:47:02.402 --> 00:47:03.552
Look, I'm...

702
00:47:03.747 --> 00:47:06.247
I'm not sure if you know this but...

703
00:47:06.676 --> 00:47:09.676
The way you are doing
your V for Victory sign.

704
00:47:10.546 --> 00:47:12.784
Well, in the poorer quarters,

705
00:47:12.987 --> 00:47:15.039
that gesture means something else.

706
00:47:15.139 --> 00:47:17.587
What does it mean?/
I wouldn’t like to say, sir.

707
00:47:17.687 --> 00:47:19.918
I was captured by the Boer,

708
00:47:20.018 --> 00:47:23.068
I spent time in a South African prison.

709
00:47:23.292 --> 00:47:24.642
Up yer’ bum.

710
00:47:26.016 --> 00:47:26.916
Sir.

711
00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:29.120
Up yer’ bum?

712
00:47:29.945 --> 00:47:30.895
Up yer’...

713
00:47:34.142 --> 00:47:36.842
The way you do it, sir. Yes sir.

714
00:47:37.591 --> 00:47:40.191
But if you turn it around, that's fine.

715
00:47:42.312 --> 00:47:45.083
Wouldn’t like millions of people
to take it the wrong way.

716
00:47:45.183 --> 00:47:46.333
Oh, I see!

717
00:47:49.624 --> 00:47:50.974
Up yer’ bum!

718
00:47:52.636 --> 00:47:53.789
Your Majesty.

719
00:47:54.420 --> 00:47:56.920
<i>Don’t wish to take too much
of your time.</i>

720
00:47:57.109 --> 00:47:58.959
<i>I heard you on the wireless.</i>

721
00:48:00.389 --> 00:48:02.139
Was I comprehensible?

722
00:48:02.942 --> 00:48:05.442
The public need to be led,

723
00:48:05.918 --> 00:48:09.523
not misled, not left to work
it out for them-themselves.

724
00:48:13.376 --> 00:48:14.226
Right.

725
00:48:15.077 --> 00:48:16.827
Will that be all, Your Majesty?

726
00:48:17.690 --> 00:48:20.040
<i>Yes. Good day Prime Minister.</i>

727
00:48:20.168 --> 00:48:21.418
Good day.

728
00:48:25.011 --> 00:48:28.462
I believe I’ve just recieved
a royal rap on the knuckles.

729
00:48:35.095 --> 00:48:37.269
As of 2200 hours last night,

730
00:48:37.369 --> 00:48:40.369
the Germans have
encircled sixty British,

731
00:48:40.564 --> 00:48:42.199
Belgian and French divisions.

732
00:48:43.829 --> 00:48:46.737
On our part all our forces
under Lord Gort,

733
00:48:46.837 --> 00:48:49.638
have now withdrawn, or are trying
to withdraw to the French coast,

734
00:48:49.639 --> 00:48:50.739
to Dunkirk.

735
00:48:51.267 --> 00:48:54.749
...where we cannot reach them./
How many of our men are trapped?

736
00:48:54.849 --> 00:48:55.649
All of them.

737
00:48:56.502 --> 00:48:59.332
Our country's entire
professional soldiery.

738
00:48:59.432 --> 00:49:02.632
And we can see
no clear way to rescue them.

739
00:49:05.143 --> 00:49:09.707
General, are you telling me
that we shall have lost

740
00:49:10.110 --> 00:49:13.610
the entire British army
by the next few days?

741
00:49:14.596 --> 00:49:15.446
That’s correct.

742
00:49:16.425 --> 00:49:19.598
The German force is
superior in every regard

743
00:49:19.698 --> 00:49:21.784
and are only fifty miles from the coast.

744
00:49:21.884 --> 00:49:23.826
They are pushing us into the sea.

745
00:49:23.926 --> 00:49:27.984
No, the Germans must not reach the sea.

746
00:49:28.107 --> 00:49:31.174
Not before we evacuate our men.

747
00:49:31.832 --> 00:49:33.382
Ismay? What have you got for us?

748
00:49:33.797 --> 00:49:36.590
As it stands, I cannot see
we have much hope of getting any

749
00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:38.640
of our forces out in time.

750
00:49:40.355 --> 00:49:41.655
Not a man?

751
00:49:43.419 --> 00:49:46.219
No, I mean, we cannot be so

752
00:49:46.374 --> 00:49:49.524
totally at their mercy!
What is our next step?

753
00:49:50.482 --> 00:49:51.382
Anyone?

754
00:49:52.659 --> 00:49:53.909
Come on! Speak!

755
00:49:56.839 --> 00:49:59.353
We still have a Garrison at Calais.
25 miles to the west.

756
00:49:59.453 --> 00:50:02.403
How many men do we have there?/
4,000. More or less.

757
00:50:05.555 --> 00:50:07.505
Why didn't you say so?

758
00:50:08.743 --> 00:50:12.103
Yes, then we have them go east,

759
00:50:12.203 --> 00:50:14.497
engage with the German
columns moving on Dunkirk.

760
00:50:14.597 --> 00:50:19.441
Buy us some time.
Draw the Nazi focus away from Dunkirk

761
00:50:19.541 --> 00:50:24.061
whilst we execute a maritime
evacuation of our forces.

762
00:50:24.161 --> 00:50:26.931
Is that possible?/
It would mean a huge sacrifice.

763
00:50:27.031 --> 00:50:31.970
Four thousand young men!/
To save 300,000!

764
00:50:32.280 --> 00:50:34.633
Under whose command
is the Calais Garrison?

765
00:50:34.733 --> 00:50:37.283
Brigadier Nicholson./  Very well.

766
00:50:39.201 --> 00:50:42.541
Tell Nicholson that it is of the
greatest importance to this island,

767
00:50:43.038 --> 00:50:45.456
that his garrison draw the enemy’s,

768
00:50:45.556 --> 00:50:48.301
tanks and artillery and
bombers away from Dunkirk.

769
00:50:48.401 --> 00:50:50.366
Invite their wrath and...

770
00:50:52.353 --> 00:50:54.253
Well, to fight on

771
00:50:55.363 --> 00:50:56.463
if needs be...

772
00:51:00.537 --> 00:51:03.737
If needs be until
the destruction of his command.

773
00:51:06.783 --> 00:51:08.033
It's suicide.

774
00:51:20.488 --> 00:51:21.738
Prime Minister.

775
00:51:22.652 --> 00:51:24.002
I have reservations.

776
00:51:24.875 --> 00:51:26.819
Who is free of reservations?

777
00:51:27.227 --> 00:51:29.277
About an unnecessary sacrifice

778
00:51:29.741 --> 00:51:32.291
when there is available alternative.

779
00:51:33.478 --> 00:51:34.778
What an alternative?

780
00:51:35.188 --> 00:51:37.384
Italy have offered
to mediate peace talks

781
00:51:37.484 --> 00:51:39.034
between ourselves and Germany.

782
00:51:39.503 --> 00:51:41.526
I already indicated that
provided the liberty

783
00:51:41.528 --> 00:51:43.638
and independence of
British Empire were assured

784
00:51:44.029 --> 00:51:45.929
we will consider any proposal.

785
00:51:46.248 --> 00:51:48.225
With... with Hitler.

786
00:51:48.736 --> 00:51:51.116
Holding the whip hand,

787
00:51:51.216 --> 00:51:54.142
do you really think he would honour
our liberty and independence?

788
00:51:54.242 --> 00:51:56.242
It would be in
his interest to do so, sir.

789
00:51:56.735 --> 00:51:58.663
Because.../  The only thing to do

790
00:51:58.763 --> 00:52:01.766
is to show that maniac

791
00:52:01.866 --> 00:52:04.612
that he cannot conquer
this island, and for that

792
00:52:04.712 --> 00:52:06.862
we need an army.
General, tell Brigadier Nicholson

793
00:52:06.962 --> 00:52:08.933
the Germans must not reach the sea!

794
00:52:09.033 --> 00:52:12.316
Not before we get our boys off that

795
00:52:12.425 --> 00:52:13.692
bloody beach!

796
00:52:13.792 --> 00:52:15.829
I take full responsibility.

797
00:52:15.929 --> 00:52:18.528
Really?/  Really! Yes, sir!

798
00:52:18.843 --> 00:52:22.684
It is the reason I sit in this chair!

799
00:52:26.114 --> 00:52:28.360
Well, surely before you
take full responsibility

800
00:52:28.361 --> 00:52:31.661
of the death of 4,000 men,
you wish to consider

801
00:52:31.761 --> 00:52:33.466
every available avenue.

802
00:52:37.181 --> 00:52:38.381
What is this?!

803
00:52:39.684 --> 00:52:42.634
What is your mind on the
principle of peace talks, sir?

804
00:52:46.452 --> 00:52:48.638
Do we take it for example, that

805
00:52:48.738 --> 00:52:50.949
you preclude yourself
from even considering

806
00:52:51.049 --> 00:52:53.154
taking part in such negotiations?

807
00:53:13.327 --> 00:53:16.912
I should like to speak
to Viscount Halifax

808
00:53:17.012 --> 00:53:20.606
and Mr. Chamberlain alone.

809
00:53:21.686 --> 00:53:24.236
Issue the order to the Calais Garrison.

810
00:53:25.338 --> 00:53:27.938
Confirm it has been done.

811
00:53:28.077 --> 00:53:29.277
Dismiss.

812
00:53:31.035 --> 00:53:32.986
You too. Come on, out.

813
00:53:35.809 --> 00:53:39.567
Bridges! You, come on, hop it!
Out! Out!

814
00:54:48.823 --> 00:54:54.243
Winston. We are facing
certain defeat on land.

815
00:54:54.545 --> 00:54:56.643
The annihilation of our army

816
00:54:56.743 --> 00:54:58.593
and imminent invasion.

817
00:54:59.624 --> 00:55:01.424
We must be rational.

818
00:55:01.946 --> 00:55:03.938
We are a sea-going nation.

819
00:55:04.270 --> 00:55:07.033
Have been since the Bronze Age.

820
00:55:08.100 --> 00:55:12.654
The Channel is ours,
our moat, our battlement

821
00:55:12.754 --> 00:55:15.636
and the German doesn’t recognise
an expanse of water

822
00:55:15.736 --> 00:55:17.693
greater than a bloody lake!

823
00:55:17.793 --> 00:55:21.171
They have first to reach
this island, Edward.

824
00:55:21.291 --> 00:55:24.090
Where are men, women, and children,
whom we will have failed

825
00:55:24.190 --> 00:55:26.384
despicably, in our duty of protection

826
00:55:26.504 --> 00:55:29.233
will be entirely defenceless./
And who's fault is that?

827
00:55:29.333 --> 00:55:32.374
Against the largest army
the world has ever seen

828
00:55:32.494 --> 00:55:34.002
Furthermore, once France falls,

829
00:55:34.102 --> 00:55:36.933
Germany can concentrate
on aircraft production

830
00:55:37.033 --> 00:55:39.200
and they will then have
the French fleet as well.

831
00:55:39.300 --> 00:55:41.979
What is to stop Hitler then, Winston?

832
00:55:42.079 --> 00:55:45.208
Words? Words? Words alone?

833
00:55:47.405 --> 00:55:49.441
If you will not permit
any talk of peace then I

834
00:55:49.541 --> 00:55:52.639
shall be forced.../
Might we not allow Edward

835
00:55:52.759 --> 00:55:56.503
simply to meet the Italian
ambassador, Bastianini,

836
00:55:56.799 --> 00:55:59.508
discuss their possible role as mediators

837
00:55:59.608 --> 00:56:01.773
between us and Germany,

838
00:56:03.346 --> 00:56:05.542
and find out their price?

839
00:56:21.805 --> 00:56:22.705
Sir.

840
00:56:43.088 --> 00:56:44.524
Mr. President.

841
00:56:44.624 --> 00:56:45.524
Winston!

842
00:56:45.721 --> 00:56:46.964
Franklin!

843
00:56:48.151 --> 00:56:49.101
How are you?

844
00:56:49.389 --> 00:56:52.354
<i>Fine. Fine.
How are you, Prime Minister?</i>

845
00:56:52.654 --> 00:56:55.554
Oh, I'm in fine, Franklin...

846
00:56:57.172 --> 00:56:58.000
Listen...

847
00:56:58.100 --> 00:57:01.863
I'm telephoning about your...

848
00:57:01.963 --> 00:57:03.363
Navy ships.

849
00:57:03.582 --> 00:57:05.582
If you could to loan us just

850
00:57:06.481 --> 00:57:10.078
50 older Destroyers.../
<i>Ah, yes.</i>

851
00:57:10.573 --> 00:57:12.423
Even 40 would do.

852
00:57:12.806 --> 00:57:13.933
<i>Well, I...</i>

853
00:57:14.599 --> 00:57:16.131
<i>I did ask around,</i>

854
00:57:17.091 --> 00:57:19.911
<i>but just not possible I’m afraid.</i>

855
00:57:20.594 --> 00:57:24.747
<i>The Neutrality Act we signed
last year has tied my hands.</i>

856
00:57:25.365 --> 00:57:26.965
<i>Just can’t swing it.</i>

857
00:57:27.128 --> 00:57:29.757
<i>I tried.</i>/  Then, can I...

858
00:57:32.226 --> 00:57:38.579
Do I have your permission
to send an aircraft carrier

859
00:57:38.679 --> 00:57:41.929
to pick up the P-40 fighter planes
we purchased from you?

860
00:57:46.209 --> 00:57:47.609
Mr. President?

861
00:57:48.635 --> 00:57:50.285
<i>You got me there again.</i>

862
00:57:51.061 --> 00:57:55.091
<i>New law preventing trans-shipment
of military equipment.</i>

863
00:57:55.363 --> 00:57:57.413
But we’ve paid for them.

864
00:57:57.874 --> 00:58:00.468
We... we've paid for them
with the money that we...

865
00:58:00.568 --> 00:58:02.168
That we borrowed from you.

866
00:58:02.357 --> 00:58:05.070
<i>I'm so, so sorry, Winston.</i>

867
00:58:09.961 --> 00:58:12.775
I need not impress upon you with

868
00:58:13.957 --> 00:58:17.644
trouble faced by the Western Hemisphere.

869
00:58:19.773 --> 00:58:23.223
Without your support, in some fashion..

870
00:58:23.323 --> 00:58:24.757
<i>I know. I know.</i>

871
00:58:25.543 --> 00:58:27.783
<i>You are on my mind day and night.</i>

872
00:58:30.744 --> 00:58:31.592
<i>Look.</i>

873
00:58:32.015 --> 00:58:34.162
<i>We could, possibly...</i>

874
00:58:34.577 --> 00:58:36.251
Mr. President.

875
00:58:36.474 --> 00:58:37.916
<i>I mean to say...</i>

876
00:58:38.016 --> 00:58:43.197
We are facing the gravest odds.

877
00:58:43.317 --> 00:58:44.896
<i>We could take your planes</i>

878
00:58:44.996 --> 00:58:47.496
<i>to about a mile from
the Canadian border,</i>

879
00:58:48.510 --> 00:58:52.755
<i>and then if you send across
a team of horses from Canada,</i>

880
00:58:53.372 --> 00:58:54.829
<i>nothing motorized,</i>

881
00:58:54.953 --> 00:58:58.453
<i>then you could pull 'em
over the border yourself.</i>

882
00:58:59.792 --> 00:59:01.263
<i>How does that sound?</i>

883
00:59:04.973 --> 00:59:05.934
Horses?

884
00:59:07.827 --> 00:59:12.487
You did say “a team of horses”?

885
00:59:13.565 --> 00:59:16.065
<i>Well, I guess you could
push ‘em yourself.</i>

886
00:59:16.873 --> 00:59:19.026
<i>The damn things have wheels.</i>

887
00:59:21.697 --> 00:59:22.676
<i>Up to you.</i>

888
00:59:26.716 --> 00:59:28.918
<i>We could do that, Prime Minister.</i>

889
00:59:32.837 --> 00:59:33.990
<i>Prime Minister?</i>

890
00:59:38.768 --> 00:59:43.426
Anything you can do at this time
would be most welcome.

891
00:59:44.314 --> 00:59:46.314
<i>Goodnight to you, Winston.</i>

892
00:59:47.159 --> 00:59:48.759
<i>It must be late there.</i>

893
00:59:50.116 --> 00:59:55.552
In more ways than
you could possibly know.

894
01:00:29.457 --> 01:00:30.707
Prime Minister.

895
01:00:30.848 --> 01:00:32.098
Prime Minister.

896
01:00:40.632 --> 01:00:43.219
Get me Admiral Ramsay on one of these.

897
01:00:43.827 --> 01:00:45.197
Yes, Prime Minister.

898
01:00:46.234 --> 01:00:47.995
Winston to Admiral Ramsay.

899
01:01:07.453 --> 01:01:08.771
Admiral Ramsay!

900
01:01:08.871 --> 01:01:10.421
It’s the Prime Minister.

901
01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:12.640
Bertie?

902
01:01:13.804 --> 01:01:14.654
Bertie.

903
01:01:15.416 --> 01:01:17.051
I hope I didn’t wake you.

904
01:01:17.151 --> 01:01:19.851
Not at all.
I was just reading the Bible.

905
01:01:20.537 --> 01:01:21.437
Exodus?

906
01:01:23.199 --> 01:01:26.961
Listen, Bertie, we need
to evacuate our boys.

907
01:01:27.710 --> 01:01:32.019
The Navy is saying that with one cruiser
and six destroyers, and with the

908
01:01:32.478 --> 01:01:34.938
Luftwaffe controlling the skies above,

909
01:01:35.038 --> 01:01:37.524
we'll be lucky to get 10% out.

910
01:01:37.624 --> 01:01:42.379
I want you to order
an assembly of boats.

911
01:01:44.732 --> 01:01:45.682
Boats?

912
01:01:46.106 --> 01:01:46.969
<i>Yes...</i>

913
01:01:47.732 --> 01:01:49.232
Civilian boats.

914
01:01:49.448 --> 01:01:51.985
As many as you can get your hands on.

915
01:01:52.205 --> 01:01:54.770
<i>Longley's clipper,
Fearnley's gin palace,</i>

916
01:01:54.870 --> 01:01:58.653
<i>anyone with a pleasure craft bigger
than 30 foot that can get to France.</i>

917
01:02:01.958 --> 01:02:03.608
<i>Bertie, you still there?</i>

918
01:02:04.885 --> 01:02:05.803
All right.

919
01:02:07.625 --> 01:02:10.658
Help me stage this thing, Bertie.

920
01:02:12.031 --> 01:02:15.931
We must at least try to bring
some of our boys home.

921
01:02:16.618 --> 01:02:19.168
I will have the BBC issue the order.

922
01:02:19.345 --> 01:02:20.195
Good.

923
01:02:21.622 --> 01:02:23.272
Bertie, you still there?

924
01:02:24.069 --> 01:02:24.972
<i>Sir?</i>

925
01:02:25.072 --> 01:02:28.849
We need a name, for this operation.

926
01:02:31.922 --> 01:02:32.866
Good morning, Prime Minister.

927
01:02:32.966 --> 01:02:35.235
Prime Minister, do you have
anything to explain this morning?

928
01:02:35.335 --> 01:02:36.861
Prime Minister, Mr. Jefferson...

929
01:02:36.961 --> 01:02:38.311
Prime Minister.

930
01:02:38.713 --> 01:02:39.696
Prime Minister!

931
01:02:39.796 --> 01:02:42.600
Prime Minister are you.../
Prime Minister! Wait!

932
01:02:49.907 --> 01:02:52.107
How do you manage
drinking during the day?

933
01:02:56.849 --> 01:02:58.399
Practice.

934
01:03:16.787 --> 01:03:17.537
I...

935
01:03:18.507 --> 01:03:20.399
Should like to discuss.

936
01:03:20.945 --> 01:03:22.395
I have been asked if

937
01:03:22.518 --> 01:03:24.360
plans should be drawn to

938
01:03:24.460 --> 01:03:28.569
evacuate myself... my family to Canada.

939
01:03:29.765 --> 01:03:32.570
I would like to know the opinion
of our Prime Minister.

940
01:03:34.046 --> 01:03:38.066
My opinion would be that you must do
what you feel is right for yourself,

941
01:03:38.955 --> 01:03:40.455
your family,

942
01:03:40.971 --> 01:03:42.221
and the nation.

943
01:03:43.461 --> 01:03:45.701
Your survival is paramount.

944
01:03:47.149 --> 01:03:48.599
Prime Ministers,

945
01:03:48.951 --> 01:03:52.301
we seem to come and go
at an astonishing rate.

946
01:03:52.882 --> 01:03:55.514
Your position in Parliament...

947
01:03:56.593 --> 01:03:58.543
I'm told isn't strong.

948
01:04:00.482 --> 01:04:01.682
My party,

949
01:04:02.899 --> 01:04:05.105
resents in the way

950
01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:08.417
Chamberlain was pushed aside.

951
01:04:08.683 --> 01:04:10.498
And many others doubt me.

952
01:04:10.598 --> 01:04:12.272
They want Halifax still.

953
01:04:12.372 --> 01:04:14.645
But why get rid of the organ grinder

954
01:04:14.745 --> 01:04:16.224
and replace him with the monkey?

955
01:04:16.225 --> 01:04:19.015
Lord Halifax is a close
personal friend of mine!

956
01:04:25.262 --> 01:04:26.827
I'm unwanted.

957
01:04:29.914 --> 01:04:33.320
I’ve never been trusted
since the Gallipoli campaign.

958
01:04:34.713 --> 01:04:35.779
Unwanted.

959
01:04:36.795 --> 01:04:39.035
Perhaps it's because you scare people.

960
01:04:39.214 --> 01:04:39.888
Who?

961
01:04:40.482 --> 01:04:41.633
You scare me.

962
01:04:42.759 --> 01:04:43.912
What?! What nonsense.

963
01:04:46.287 --> 01:04:49.531
What could possibly be scary about me?

964
01:04:49.715 --> 01:04:52.560
One never knows what is going to
come out of your mouth next.

965
01:04:52.725 --> 01:04:56.315
Something that will flatter,
something that will wound.

966
01:05:01.690 --> 01:05:05.469
My emotions are unbridled.

967
01:05:07.072 --> 01:05:09.612
A wildness. In the blood.

968
01:05:09.712 --> 01:05:12.850
I share it with my father.

969
01:05:14.127 --> 01:05:15.692
My mother also.

970
01:05:16.893 --> 01:05:20.176
We lack the gift of temperance.

971
01:05:22.395 --> 01:05:24.113
Were you close to your parents?

972
01:05:24.213 --> 01:05:27.511
My mother was glamorous.

973
01:05:29.075 --> 01:05:31.325
But perhaps too widely loved.

974
01:05:33.085 --> 01:05:35.539
My father was like God.

975
01:05:36.619 --> 01:05:38.076
Busy elsewhere.

976
01:06:21.888 --> 01:06:23.388
Good day, gentlemen.

977
01:06:23.532 --> 01:06:28.348
I’ve asked the Minister for War
to join us.

978
01:06:30.165 --> 01:06:31.515
Neville, you sit on there.

979
01:06:32.270 --> 01:06:33.170
Anthony.

980
01:06:35.695 --> 01:06:40.371
Obviously, we are in
a dangerous situation.

981
01:06:40.848 --> 01:06:43.762
But I am assured by the French Premiere
that while some

982
01:06:44.868 --> 01:06:48.568
German tank units have reached the sea,

983
01:06:48.668 --> 01:06:51.647
the situation generally seems
to be well in hand.

984
01:06:51.854 --> 01:06:53.893
What news from Calais?

985
01:06:54.013 --> 01:06:55.767
The Garrison attacked,
but was forced back,

986
01:06:55.867 --> 01:06:58.317
and is now surrounded on all sides.

987
01:06:59.046 --> 01:07:01.720
They are being shelled
and bombed mercilessly.

988
01:07:01.877 --> 01:07:04.227
Casualties are at sixty percent.

989
01:07:14.899 --> 01:07:16.052
Prime Minister.

990
01:07:16.911 --> 01:07:19.209
The question of peace talks.

991
01:07:19.707 --> 01:07:21.686
We must hold our nerve.

992
01:07:22.085 --> 01:07:26.101
Signal only that we're intend
to fight it out into the end.

993
01:07:26.821 --> 01:07:30.892
A peace offer telegraphs
our weakness./  Agreed.

994
01:07:30.992 --> 01:07:32.214
And even if we were beaten,

995
01:07:32.314 --> 01:07:35.972
we should be no worse off than we should be
if we were now to abandon the struggle.

996
01:07:36.529 --> 01:07:39.487
Let us therefore avoid
being dragged down

997
01:07:39.587 --> 01:07:43.798
the slippery slope
with talk of a negotiated peace.

998
01:07:43.898 --> 01:07:45.198
Slippery slope?

999
01:07:45.298 --> 01:07:47.676
The only.../
I suspect Italy and Germany...

1000
01:07:47.776 --> 01:07:51.296
The only slippery slope.../  ...want to
get us so deeply involved in negotiations

1001
01:07:51.396 --> 01:07:53.924
that we should be unable to turn back.

1002
01:07:54.024 --> 01:07:55.642
Nonsense! Bastianini informed me...

1003
01:07:55.742 --> 01:07:57.541
I propose.../
The only slippery slope...

1004
01:07:57.641 --> 01:08:01.242
Will you stop interrupting me
while I am interrupting you?!

1005
01:08:05.236 --> 01:08:06.805
When I chose my War Cabinet,

1006
01:08:06.905 --> 01:08:10.166
I took great care to surround
myself with old rivals.

1007
01:08:11.630 --> 01:08:13.100
I may have overdone it.

1008
01:08:14.166 --> 01:08:18.102
Viscount Halifax,
the approach you proposed is...

1009
01:08:18.321 --> 01:08:21.105
It's not only... it's futile,

1010
01:08:21.879 --> 01:08:24.000
but involves us in a deadly danger.

1011
01:08:24.161 --> 01:08:29.835
The deadly danger here is this
romantic fantasy of fighting to the end.

1012
01:08:31.883 --> 01:08:33.233
What is “the end”?

1013
01:08:34.130 --> 01:08:36.573
If not the destruction of all thing?

1014
01:08:37.485 --> 01:08:39.606
There is nothing heroic
in going down fighting

1015
01:08:39.706 --> 01:08:41.156
if it can be avoided.

1016
01:08:41.313 --> 01:08:44.861
Nothing even remotely patriotic
in death or glory

1017
01:08:44.961 --> 01:08:47.069
if the odds are on the former.

1018
01:08:47.169 --> 01:08:50.145
Nothing inglorious in trying
to shorten a war

1019
01:08:50.245 --> 01:08:52.886
that we are clearly losing./  Losing?

1020
01:08:52.986 --> 01:08:55.836
Europe is still.../  Europe is lost!

1021
01:09:03.102 --> 01:09:06.702
And before our forces
are wiped out completely,

1022
01:09:07.252 --> 01:09:09.102
now is the time to negotiate,

1023
01:09:09.315 --> 01:09:12.515
in order to obtain the
best conditions possible.

1024
01:09:13.880 --> 01:09:16.368
Hitler would not interests to insist
on outrageous terms.

1025
01:09:16.468 --> 01:09:18.218
He will know his own weaknesses.

1026
01:09:18.617 --> 01:09:21.626
He will be reasonable./
When will the lesson be learned?

1027
01:09:21.726 --> 01:09:24.100
When will the lesson be learned?

1028
01:09:25.423 --> 01:09:31.383
How many more dictators must be
wooed, appeased...

1029
01:09:31.483 --> 01:09:33.401
Good God, given immense privileges,

1030
01:09:33.501 --> 01:09:35.130
before we learn.

1031
01:09:35.798 --> 01:09:41.443
You can’t reason with a tiger
when your head is in it's mouth!

1032
01:09:43.456 --> 01:09:44.706
Prime Minister!

1033
01:09:45.353 --> 01:09:46.253
Winston!

1034
01:09:46.720 --> 01:09:47.620
Winston!

1035
01:09:48.682 --> 01:09:51.557
Yesterday you gave permission for.../
What permission?

1036
01:09:51.657 --> 01:09:53.242
For me to meet Bastianini.

1037
01:09:53.342 --> 01:09:56.246
I sanctioned theoretical exploration.../
Theoretical?

1038
01:09:56.346 --> 01:09:58.346
...of what price Italy would ask.

1039
01:09:58.446 --> 01:10:00.441
Nothing more. I did not sanction.../
If you will not allow any further

1040
01:10:00.541 --> 01:10:02.458
exploration of a peace agreement,

1041
01:10:02.558 --> 01:10:04.322
then you will have my resignation.

1042
01:10:04.422 --> 01:10:05.643
Oh, don’t be absurd.

1043
01:10:05.743 --> 01:10:09.132
I need you, Edward, you know I do./
I will not stand by

1044
01:10:09.383 --> 01:10:11.925
to watch another generation of young men

1045
01:10:12.025 --> 01:10:14.699
die at the bloody altar of your hubris!

1046
01:10:14.799 --> 01:10:17.082
And you would have us die as lambs!

1047
01:10:17.197 --> 01:10:18.664
Was Gallipoli not enough for you?

1048
01:10:18.764 --> 01:10:20.114
How dare you!

1049
01:10:20.691 --> 01:10:24.274
Our troops were chewing
barbed wire in Flanders!

1050
01:10:24.374 --> 01:10:26.074
And I saw it!

1051
01:10:26.831 --> 01:10:30.542
Opening a second front,
outflanking the Turks was

1052
01:10:30.768 --> 01:10:34.521
a serious military idea and
it could’ve damn-well worked if

1053
01:10:34.621 --> 01:10:36.664
if the Admirals and the First Sea Lord

1054
01:10:36.764 --> 01:10:39.814
hadn’t dithered away
our element of surprise!

1055
01:10:44.457 --> 01:10:46.257
The choice is yours, Winston.

1056
01:10:46.734 --> 01:10:50.384
You have 24 hours to enter
into peace talks, or I resign.

1057
01:11:04.563 --> 01:11:05.716
I told him.

1058
01:11:06.646 --> 01:11:08.016
It shook him.

1059
01:11:08.524 --> 01:11:09.636
Imagine it did.

1060
01:11:10.320 --> 01:11:11.813
Gave him 24 hours.

1061
01:11:11.933 --> 01:11:13.648
I don’t expect him to agree,

1062
01:11:13.748 --> 01:11:15.941
so I should resign first.
You then follow.

1063
01:11:16.041 --> 01:11:19.616
That’s critical if we are to trigger
a revolt to new confirm of the House.

1064
01:11:19.984 --> 01:11:21.334
I should announce it.

1065
01:11:23.174 --> 01:11:24.674
The King will back us.

1066
01:11:41.437 --> 01:11:42.590
Prime Minister.

1067
01:11:42.836 --> 01:11:45.902
I wonder if we might schedule you
to address the Outer Cabinet?

1068
01:11:47.306 --> 01:11:49.913
<i>Today, the Admiralty have made
an Order requesting</i>

1069
01:11:50.013 --> 01:11:52.465
<i>all owners of self-propelled
pleasure craft,</i>

1070
01:11:52.565 --> 01:11:55.077
<i>between 30' and 100' in length,</i>

1071
01:11:55.197 --> 01:11:58.515
<i>to send all particulars
to the Admiralty, immediately.</i>

1072
01:11:58.615 --> 01:12:00.342
To Brigadier Nicholson.

1073
01:12:03.901 --> 01:12:05.880
30th Infantry Brigade.

1074
01:12:07.096 --> 01:12:07.901
Calais.

1075
01:12:11.104 --> 01:12:12.154
Every hour

1076
01:12:13.574 --> 01:12:15.874
you continue to exist

1077
01:12:17.144 --> 01:12:18.944
is of the greatest help

1078
01:12:20.075 --> 01:12:22.155
to our forces at Dunkirk.

1079
01:12:25.354 --> 01:12:26.804
Have the greatest

1080
01:12:27.528 --> 01:12:28.578
possible

1081
01:12:28.871 --> 01:12:29.971
admiration

1082
01:12:31.120 --> 01:12:33.300
for your splendid stand.

1083
01:12:38.321 --> 01:12:40.517
Your evacuation however

1084
01:12:41.308 --> 01:12:43.480
will not take place.

1085
01:12:49.689 --> 01:12:50.639
I repeat...

1086
01:12:53.523 --> 01:12:54.873
Will not take place.

1087
01:13:03.709 --> 01:13:04.709
Signed.

1088
01:13:20.436 --> 01:13:21.286
Here.

1089
01:13:21.485 --> 01:13:22.335
Here!

1090
01:13:25.872 --> 01:13:27.222
May I be excused?

1091
01:13:27.556 --> 01:13:28.506
You may not!

1092
01:13:30.920 --> 01:13:32.270
What is this about?

1093
01:13:35.243 --> 01:13:37.193
No one tells us anything.

1094
01:13:38.813 --> 01:13:40.813
It’s all classified.

1095
01:13:42.524 --> 01:13:45.924
We hear scraps and it’s worse
than knowing nothing.

1096
01:13:50.074 --> 01:13:51.624
What would you like to know?

1097
01:13:59.510 --> 01:14:01.811
How many men will survive?

1098
01:14:09.111 --> 01:14:10.261
Come with me.

1099
01:14:18.874 --> 01:14:21.164
I’m not allowed in the Map Room./
You are now.

1100
01:14:21.264 --> 01:14:22.452
Prime Minister.

1101
01:14:22.552 --> 01:14:24.899
Prime Minister./  At ease, gentlemen.

1102
01:14:26.450 --> 01:14:27.375
Now.

1103
01:14:30.339 --> 01:14:33.190
The German army controls
every French port,

1104
01:14:33.310 --> 01:14:35.747
except Dunkirk, here, and
Calais, here to the west,

1105
01:14:35.847 --> 01:14:38.401
where the garrison
under Brigadier Nicholson,

1106
01:14:38.501 --> 01:14:42.480
is drawing fire and delaying
the German advance on Dunkirk.

1107
01:14:42.707 --> 01:14:44.207
At both points,

1108
01:14:44.565 --> 01:14:46.765
our troops are encircled.

1109
01:14:47.065 --> 01:14:50.193
We’re still trying to
clear Dunkirk harbour,

1110
01:14:50.293 --> 01:14:53.070
of wrecked ships so we can
then land the boats we need

1111
01:14:53.170 --> 01:14:55.972
to get our boys off those beaches, but

1112
01:14:56.072 --> 01:14:58.374
enemy aircraft is
attacking us constantly.

1113
01:14:58.474 --> 01:15:01.098
Our only hope in Dunkirk is

1114
01:15:01.198 --> 01:15:03.198
thick cloud cover

1115
01:15:03.359 --> 01:15:06.559
to thwart these attacks,
but the skies remain clear.

1116
01:15:07.131 --> 01:15:08.098
Even then,

1117
01:15:08.889 --> 01:15:11.277
I am told we will need

1118
01:15:11.377 --> 01:15:15.327
a miracle to get even
10% of our men out.

1119
01:15:17.956 --> 01:15:20.077
Courage, Miss Layton.

1120
01:15:21.691 --> 01:15:22.641
Courage.

1121
01:15:23.404 --> 01:15:24.354
General.

1122
01:15:24.862 --> 01:15:27.112
How long have they got,

1123
01:15:28.609 --> 01:15:30.409
if we don’t rescue them?

1124
01:15:33.442 --> 01:15:34.142
One,

1125
01:15:35.033 --> 01:15:36.033
maybe...

1126
01:15:37.352 --> 01:15:38.402
Two days.

1127
01:15:44.558 --> 01:15:47.257
News from Calais.
The 13th Infantry, sir.

1128
01:15:47.357 --> 01:15:49.052
They’ve retreated to the town’s citadel.

1129
01:15:49.053 --> 01:15:51.802
As a last and probably hopeless stand.

1130
01:15:51.902 --> 01:15:54.552
The order “Everyman for himself"
has been given.

1131
01:15:54.838 --> 01:15:57.888
Brigadier Nicholson, there's
a telegram, sir./  Thank you.

1132
01:16:03.175 --> 01:16:04.175
All right.

1133
01:16:04.578 --> 01:16:06.278
Let's do this, well done.

1134
01:16:21.807 --> 01:16:24.157
All right, man?/  All right.

1135
01:16:36.674 --> 01:16:39.124
<i>Your evacuation however</i>

1136
01:16:39.611 --> 01:16:41.978
<i>will not take place.</i>

1137
01:16:44.071 --> 01:16:46.906
<i>I repeat, it will not take place.</i>

1138
01:17:14.100 --> 01:17:15.990
<i>CALAIS FALLEN.</i>

1139
01:17:16.090 --> 01:17:19.297
<i>NUMBERS OF DEAD OR CAPTURED UNCERTAIN.
SIGNED BY GENERAL GORT.</i>

1140
01:17:34.445 --> 01:17:35.989
Go to bed, Pig.

1141
01:17:37.949 --> 01:17:39.099
You must sleep.

1142
01:17:42.189 --> 01:17:43.697
Leave me, Clemmie.

1143
01:17:44.446 --> 01:17:47.846
The opportunity for doing so
passed a long time ago.

1144
01:18:18.903 --> 01:18:19.953
<i>Operator.</i>

1145
01:18:20.126 --> 01:18:21.570
<i>How do I direct your call?</i>

1146
01:18:21.670 --> 01:18:23.770
Get me Ramsay./  <i>One minute.</i>

1147
01:18:25.877 --> 01:18:27.477
Bertie?/  <i>Sir?</i>

1148
01:18:27.577 --> 01:18:29.077
What’s been the response?

1149
01:18:29.177 --> 01:18:30.483
<i>It will take time.</i>

1150
01:18:30.583 --> 01:18:32.117
<i>It’s too soon to judge.</i>

1151
01:18:32.118 --> 01:18:33.614
How many boats so far?

1152
01:18:33.714 --> 01:18:35.361
<i>Winston, it will take time.</i>

1153
01:18:35.461 --> 01:18:38.911
The request for civilian boats, Bertie,
was not a request.

1154
01:18:39.178 --> 01:18:40.736
It was an order!

1155
01:18:57.234 --> 01:18:59.284
Miss Layton./  Sir.

1156
01:18:59.384 --> 01:19:01.293
I need to reach...

1157
01:19:01.816 --> 01:19:03.216
To reach...

1158
01:19:03.745 --> 01:19:05.095
Speak to...

1159
01:19:07.103 --> 01:19:07.777
To?

1160
01:19:08.712 --> 01:19:09.559
3pm.

1161
01:19:09.659 --> 01:19:11.009
3pm, sir?

1162
01:19:12.091 --> 01:19:12.791
Yes.

1163
01:19:13.887 --> 01:19:16.369
Yes. Ask Bridges to summon the uh...

1164
01:19:18.415 --> 01:19:22.195
The War Cabinet at 3pm. Thank you.

1165
01:19:26.732 --> 01:19:27.632
Sir.

1166
01:19:28.386 --> 01:19:30.586
From Lord Gort, in France, sir.

1167
01:19:35.903 --> 01:19:37.771
Belgium has fallen.

1168
01:19:39.978 --> 01:19:43.243
They will surrender at midnight.

1169
01:19:46.262 --> 01:19:48.371
France will soon follow suit.

1170
01:20:01.196 --> 01:20:03.977
We have received reports that
the most probable method attack

1171
01:20:04.077 --> 01:20:07.212
which Germany might employ
against this country

1172
01:20:07.312 --> 01:20:09.535
is a large fleet of fast motor boats,

1173
01:20:09.635 --> 01:20:11.131
possibly up to 200,

1174
01:20:11.231 --> 01:20:13.676
carrying 100 men apiece
to carry out a seaborne raid

1175
01:20:13.776 --> 01:20:15.026
on a large scale.

1176
01:20:15.448 --> 01:20:17.571
By this means a considerable
force of the enemy

1177
01:20:17.671 --> 01:20:19.795
could be landed
at many points on the coast

1178
01:20:19.895 --> 01:20:22.895
simultaneously with
air-borne raids inland.

1179
01:20:23.328 --> 01:20:26.787
We do not feel that
by naval or air action

1180
01:20:26.887 --> 01:20:29.287
we could prevent such a landing.

1181
01:20:31.708 --> 01:20:33.108
Could you repeat that?

1182
01:20:33.883 --> 01:20:37.231
We must prepare for the imminent
invasion of our island.

1183
01:20:40.923 --> 01:20:44.273
We must prepare for the imminent
invasion of our island.

1184
01:20:44.426 --> 01:20:46.871
We recommend that
the country should be warned

1185
01:20:46.971 --> 01:20:49.136
and drawn to
the immediate danger,

1186
01:20:49.236 --> 01:20:51.747
and that all personnel
require to put Britain

1187
01:20:51.748 --> 01:20:56.768
in a state of defense should be
mobilized. Wtihout a moment delayed.

1188
01:20:57.151 --> 01:20:57.901
Lights.

1189
01:21:04.061 --> 01:21:05.096
Thank you.

1190
01:21:06.044 --> 01:21:06.937
General.

1191
01:21:09.841 --> 01:21:10.853
That all...

1192
01:21:11.625 --> 01:21:13.634
preparations be made.

1193
01:21:15.124 --> 01:21:18.820
Let the record state I have received
word from the Italian Embassy in London.

1194
01:21:18.920 --> 01:21:20.704
Italy is prepared
to mediate a resolution

1195
01:21:20.804 --> 01:21:23.804
between Britain, it's allies,
and Germany.

1196
01:21:30.323 --> 01:21:31.783
Perhaps... then...

1197
01:21:34.206 --> 01:21:38.484
The time for such an offer from us is...

1198
01:21:38.584 --> 01:21:40.205
is when...

1199
01:21:45.659 --> 01:21:47.109
is when...

1200
01:21:49.101 --> 01:21:51.294
Is when Germany has made

1201
01:21:51.394 --> 01:21:54.529
an unsuccessful attempt

1202
01:21:54.649 --> 01:21:56.036
to invade this country.

1203
01:21:56.136 --> 01:21:58.462
Unsuccessful?/
Then you leave me no other option

1204
01:21:58.562 --> 01:22:01.436
to offer.../  Winston! Winston!

1205
01:22:01.860 --> 01:22:05.912
You are refusing
to grasp the realities of

1206
01:22:06.863 --> 01:22:09.563
how precarious our position is!

1207
01:22:10.047 --> 01:22:13.748
Our entire army
is about to be wiped out.

1208
01:22:14.209 --> 01:22:15.216
Terms!

1209
01:22:15.336 --> 01:22:17.057
Must! Be struck!

1210
01:22:22.565 --> 01:22:23.462
Anthony?

1211
01:22:42.047 --> 01:22:44.161
Then it seems...

1212
01:22:46.476 --> 01:22:48.121
We have no choice,

1213
01:22:49.454 --> 01:22:51.521
but to at least consider the

1214
01:22:52.806 --> 01:22:53.756
path

1215
01:22:54.613 --> 01:22:56.177
of negotiation.

1216
01:22:57.711 --> 01:23:00.922
If Hitler's peace terms are...

1217
01:23:03.062 --> 01:23:06.785
Overlordship of Central Europe

1218
01:23:08.360 --> 01:23:11.602
and return of certain
German colonies...

1219
01:23:12.689 --> 01:23:14.004
A-a-and...

1220
01:23:15.031 --> 01:23:16.720
If he will leave us

1221
01:23:18.160 --> 01:23:19.910
our independence,

1222
01:23:22.817 --> 01:23:25.652
then I'll be greatful

1223
01:23:26.881 --> 01:23:29.081
to get out of our present

1224
01:23:30.976 --> 01:23:32.076
difficulties.

1225
01:23:34.038 --> 01:23:35.938
It's quite unlikely

1226
01:23:36.702 --> 01:23:39.533
that he will make such an offer,

1227
01:23:39.633 --> 01:23:44.572
but if I were told what
the German terms were,

1228
01:23:49.516 --> 01:23:51.502
then I would be prepared

1229
01:23:51.847 --> 01:23:53.847
to consider them.

1230
01:23:55.609 --> 01:23:57.286
Thank you, Prime Minister.

1231
01:23:58.849 --> 01:24:01.333
We shall prepare a draft
memorandum at once.

1232
01:24:13.496 --> 01:24:17.524
I have come to wonder,

1233
01:24:18.045 --> 01:24:20.396
in recent days,

1234
01:24:25.383 --> 01:24:27.769
whether it was

1235
01:24:30.697 --> 01:24:32.197
part of my duty...

1236
01:24:37.349 --> 01:24:40.521
Part of my...
Part of my duty, yeah.

1237
01:24:44.604 --> 01:24:45.804
To consider...

1238
01:24:47.776 --> 01:24:48.976
To consider...

1239
01:24:56.083 --> 01:24:57.583
To consider...

1240
01:25:00.303 --> 01:25:02.741
Whether it was...

1241
01:25:03.685 --> 01:25:06.235
Will that be all for tonight, sir?

1242
01:25:09.789 --> 01:25:12.348
Whether it was part of my duty.

1243
01:25:14.063 --> 01:25:16.709
Entering into...

1244
01:25:18.751 --> 01:25:20.985
Into negotiations with...

1245
01:25:21.498 --> 01:25:26.123
<i>HITLER DEFIANT
RUTHLESS IN ATTACK</i>

1246
01:25:26.353 --> 01:25:27.203
that...

1247
01:25:29.746 --> 01:25:31.046
that corporal...

1248
01:25:36.311 --> 01:25:36.961
No.

1249
01:25:37.413 --> 01:25:39.133
That tyrant...

1250
01:25:43.061 --> 01:25:46.867
Monster of wickedness...

1251
01:25:48.586 --> 01:25:50.226
Butcher... that...

1252
01:25:51.162 --> 01:25:53.402
That monstrous...

1253
01:25:54.113 --> 01:25:55.428
Savage.

1254
01:25:58.700 --> 01:26:07.083
Bloodthirsty guttersnipe!
Housepainter...

1255
01:26:11.373 --> 01:26:12.773
HOUSEPAINTER!

1256
01:26:20.822 --> 01:26:22.372
Where were we?

1257
01:26:26.336 --> 01:26:27.186
Well?!

1258
01:26:30.281 --> 01:26:32.031
I didn’t understand you, sir.

1259
01:26:33.878 --> 01:26:34.978
You were...

1260
01:26:35.787 --> 01:26:36.587
Were?

1261
01:26:37.700 --> 01:26:39.100
You were mumbling.

1262
01:26:39.666 --> 01:26:40.766
Mumbling.

1263
01:26:46.442 --> 01:26:48.719
The... the... the right words...

1264
01:26:50.570 --> 01:26:52.170
Wouldn't come.

1265
01:26:52.728 --> 01:26:54.359
They will come, sir.

1266
01:26:54.749 --> 01:26:57.749
No one compared
what's together like you.

1267
01:27:20.412 --> 01:27:21.812
Your beau?

1268
01:27:23.027 --> 01:27:24.177
My brother.

1269
01:27:27.116 --> 01:27:28.166
Where is he now?

1270
01:27:29.408 --> 01:27:31.408
He was falling back on Dunkirk.

1271
01:27:33.023 --> 01:27:34.772
But he never made it.

1272
01:27:59.304 --> 01:28:00.254
What?

1273
01:28:05.839 --> 01:28:07.439
I'm just looking at you.

1274
01:28:34.719 --> 01:28:37.319
Shall I read back what we have?

1275
01:28:38.822 --> 01:28:40.372
Yes. Yes please.

1276
01:28:47.293 --> 01:28:50.343
I have come to wonder in recent days

1277
01:28:51.437 --> 01:28:55.512
whether it was my duty to consider
entering into negotiations

1278
01:28:57.123 --> 01:28:57.823
with...

1279
01:29:01.626 --> 01:29:02.426
With...

1280
01:29:51.532 --> 01:29:54.251
I just couldn't standing... imagining

1281
01:29:55.737 --> 01:29:57.737
never being here anymore.

1282
01:29:59.022 --> 01:30:03.091
Whether is because I am no longer alive,
or the palace itself is gone.

1283
01:30:04.203 --> 01:30:05.053
Canada.

1284
01:30:06.417 --> 01:30:08.067
You must decide, sir.

1285
01:30:08.517 --> 01:30:10.339
You could rule in exile./  Truly...

1286
01:30:10.439 --> 01:30:12.339
...is that to be my fate?

1287
01:30:17.795 --> 01:30:20.267
You know something
I’m aware in this moment?

1288
01:30:20.367 --> 01:30:22.211
Still in hate-grade.

1289
01:30:25.009 --> 01:30:26.509
Bloody angry.

1290
01:30:30.438 --> 01:30:31.288
Darling?

1291
01:30:34.795 --> 01:30:35.995
Winston, Darling?

1292
01:30:39.216 --> 01:30:41.366
There's someone to see you.

1293
01:30:41.652 --> 01:30:42.852
You have a visitor.

1294
01:30:54.693 --> 01:30:58.143
My darling, you’ve the full weight
of the world on your shoulders.

1295
01:30:59.070 --> 01:31:01.170
I.../  No, I know, I know.

1296
01:31:01.392 --> 01:31:03.742
But these inner battles

1297
01:31:04.927 --> 01:31:05.977
have actually

1298
01:31:06.539 --> 01:31:07.689
trained you

1299
01:31:08.074 --> 01:31:09.924
for this very moment.

1300
01:31:10.355 --> 01:31:13.184
You are strong because
you are imperfect.

1301
01:31:13.284 --> 01:31:16.890
You are wise because you have doubts.

1302
01:31:19.590 --> 01:31:22.090
Now, should I let him in?

1303
01:31:23.908 --> 01:31:24.608
Who?

1304
01:31:25.821 --> 01:31:26.750
The king.

1305
01:31:27.940 --> 01:31:28.890
Which King?

1306
01:31:29.921 --> 01:31:31.507
Our King?

1307
01:31:31.964 --> 01:31:34.800
Well if it isn’t him, it’s
a most impersonation.

1308
01:31:44.570 --> 01:31:45.920
Mr. Churchill.

1309
01:31:46.670 --> 01:31:49.220
I hope you can forgive
the late our hours.

1310
01:31:49.854 --> 01:31:53.304
Your wife thought tonight
would be a good time.

1311
01:31:54.883 --> 01:31:56.133
Shall we sit?

1312
01:31:56.444 --> 01:31:57.895
Oh... oh, yes...

1313
01:31:58.442 --> 01:31:59.242
Please.

1314
01:32:04.273 --> 01:32:06.873
Something to drink? Perhaps?

1315
01:32:14.202 --> 01:32:15.245
I...

1316
01:32:16.307 --> 01:32:17.507
received...

1317
01:32:18.361 --> 01:32:19.461
a visit...

1318
01:32:20.793 --> 01:32:23.146
From?/  Viscount Halifax.

1319
01:32:23.579 --> 01:32:26.529
It appears the prospect of a peace deal

1320
01:32:27.864 --> 01:32:29.464
has increased

1321
01:32:30.196 --> 01:32:31.196
dramatically.

1322
01:32:32.962 --> 01:32:35.814
The war cabinet is drafting a letter

1323
01:32:35.914 --> 01:32:37.014
to Mussolini,

1324
01:32:38.082 --> 01:32:40.810
asking him to broker talks

1325
01:32:41.560 --> 01:32:42.860
with Herr Hitler.

1326
01:32:46.456 --> 01:32:47.806
Then Halifax

1327
01:32:48.235 --> 01:32:49.435
was correct?

1328
01:32:51.627 --> 01:32:53.900
I should like to know your mind.

1329
01:32:54.334 --> 01:32:56.834
It would be helpful to know yours first.

1330
01:32:57.515 --> 01:33:00.465
Mine? I should like to know it myself.

1331
01:33:02.544 --> 01:33:03.694
Nations

1332
01:33:05.010 --> 01:33:08.310
which go down fighting rise again,
and those

1333
01:33:09.305 --> 01:33:12.005
which surrender tamely are finished.

1334
01:33:12.228 --> 01:33:13.228
Belgium?

1335
01:33:13.909 --> 01:33:14.677
Collapsed.

1336
01:33:14.777 --> 01:33:16.377
Norway?/  Holland.

1337
01:33:17.458 --> 01:33:18.850
France any hour.

1338
01:33:18.950 --> 01:33:21.571
And the mood of Parliament?/
Fear. Panic.

1339
01:33:21.671 --> 01:33:22.471
And you?

1340
01:33:24.985 --> 01:33:26.335
Are you not afraid?

1341
01:33:28.214 --> 01:33:31.397
I am most terribly.

1342
01:33:32.959 --> 01:33:37.333
Support in the War Cabinet,

1343
01:33:38.738 --> 01:33:43.381
for a campaign of resistance
has collapsed.

1344
01:33:51.833 --> 01:33:55.133
Later today, I will address the House

1345
01:33:55.983 --> 01:33:57.233
accordingly.

1346
01:34:08.217 --> 01:34:09.867
You have my support.

1347
01:34:17.307 --> 01:34:18.557
Your Majesty?

1348
01:34:19.923 --> 01:34:21.573
You have my support.

1349
01:34:24.306 --> 01:34:25.706
I confess I

1350
01:34:27.066 --> 01:34:29.866
had some reservations
about you at first, but

1351
01:34:31.247 --> 01:34:35.611
while some in this country
dreaded your appointment, none...

1352
01:34:35.731 --> 01:34:38.531
None dreaded it like Adolph Hitler.

1353
01:34:38.634 --> 01:34:41.934
Whomever can strike fear
into that brute heart

1354
01:34:43.089 --> 01:34:45.383
is worthy of all our trust.

1355
01:34:46.301 --> 01:34:50.151
We shall work together. You shall
have my support. At any hour.

1356
01:34:51.117 --> 01:34:52.617
Beat the buggers.

1357
01:34:54.106 --> 01:34:55.656
Now I'm going to Parliament,

1358
01:34:56.664 --> 01:34:59.464
but without support in my own party.

1359
01:35:00.099 --> 01:35:02.010
I must sue for peace.

1360
01:35:06.411 --> 01:35:10.361
You once gave me some advice.
Perhaps I can give you some.

1361
01:35:11.692 --> 01:35:12.992
Go to the people.

1362
01:35:13.237 --> 01:35:15.270
Let them instruct you.

1363
01:35:15.572 --> 01:35:18.372
Quite silently, they usually do.

1364
01:35:19.121 --> 01:35:23.863
But tell them the truth. Unvarnished.

1365
01:35:24.654 --> 01:35:26.648
If invasion is imminent,

1366
01:35:26.748 --> 01:35:29.432
then our troops in France are lost,

1367
01:35:29.532 --> 01:35:31.082
they must be prepare.

1368
01:35:34.702 --> 01:35:36.252
On certain matters,

1369
01:35:38.386 --> 01:35:42.288
I have value few people with whom I can

1370
01:35:43.382 --> 01:35:44.382
talk,

1371
01:35:44.667 --> 01:35:45.768
frankly.

1372
01:35:45.868 --> 01:35:48.586
Perhaps now we have each other?

1373
01:35:49.576 --> 01:35:51.376
And I no longer scare you?

1374
01:35:52.524 --> 01:35:53.374
A little.

1375
01:35:54.330 --> 01:35:55.880
I can cope.

1376
01:36:05.641 --> 01:36:06.791
We are ready.

1377
01:36:07.022 --> 01:36:08.122
More or less.

1378
01:36:08.283 --> 01:36:10.355
<i>More or less what, Bertie?</i>

1379
01:36:10.659 --> 01:36:11.959
<i>Give me a number.</i>

1380
01:36:12.260 --> 01:36:15.000
In total 860 vessels.

1381
01:36:15.504 --> 01:36:17.895
<i>The biggest civilian fleet
ever assembled.</i>

1382
01:36:17.995 --> 01:36:20.314
<i>Operation Dynamo waits on your command.</i>

1383
01:36:20.553 --> 01:36:21.703
Admiral...

1384
01:36:22.983 --> 01:36:25.243
Initiate Dynamo.

1385
01:36:26.085 --> 01:36:28.130
<i>And may God watch over them all.</i>

1386
01:37:53.393 --> 01:37:55.543
We’ve lost the Prime Minister!

1387
01:38:17.465 --> 01:38:20.465
Do you know how to
use this thing?/  Yes, sir.

1388
01:38:22.049 --> 01:38:24.249
How do I get to Westminster?

1389
01:38:24.397 --> 01:38:25.497
Westminster?

1390
01:38:27.180 --> 01:38:29.558
The District Line. East.
One stop.

1391
01:38:29.678 --> 01:38:32.636
District Line. East. One stop.

1392
01:38:33.406 --> 01:38:35.756
That doesn’t sound so hard./  No, sir.

1393
01:38:36.768 --> 01:38:37.618
Thank you.

1394
01:38:38.078 --> 01:38:38.989
Thank you, sir.

1395
01:38:39.089 --> 01:38:43.436
The Memorandum is titled
“Suggested Approaches to Italy”

1396
01:38:43.536 --> 01:38:44.936
for is as follows.

1397
01:38:45.238 --> 01:38:48.461
“If Signor Mussolini
will co-operate with us

1398
01:38:48.902 --> 01:38:52.549
in securing a settlement
of all European questions, which

1399
01:38:52.802 --> 01:38:56.543
safeguard the independence
and security of the allies,

1400
01:38:56.663 --> 01:38:58.551
and could be the basis

1401
01:38:58.651 --> 01:39:01.520
of a just and durable peace for Europe.”

1402
01:39:01.620 --> 01:39:04.800
“We will undertake at once to discuss

1403
01:39:05.257 --> 01:39:07.418
with the desire to find solutions,

1404
01:39:07.518 --> 01:39:10.065
the matters in which Signor Mussolini

1405
01:39:10.165 --> 01:39:12.143
is primarily interested.”

1406
01:39:12.263 --> 01:39:13.695
<i>“We understand</i>

1407
01:39:13.795 --> 01:39:17.926
<i>that he desires the solution
of certain Mediterranean questions</i>

1408
01:39:18.026 --> 01:39:22.220
<i>and if he will state in secrecy
what these are,</i>

1409
01:39:22.320 --> 01:39:25.689
<i>France and Great Britain
will at once do their best</i>

1410
01:39:25.789 --> 01:39:27.810
<i>to meet those wishes.”</i>

1411
01:39:33.186 --> 01:39:34.186
So...

1412
01:39:34.970 --> 01:39:37.093
This is the Underground!

1413
01:39:49.732 --> 01:39:50.582
Thank you.

1414
01:40:29.450 --> 01:40:31.900
Does anyone have a match?

1415
01:40:37.131 --> 01:40:37.981
Thank you.

1416
01:40:40.025 --> 01:40:40.875
Thank you.

1417
01:40:44.335 --> 01:40:45.834
What are you all staring at?

1418
01:40:46.329 --> 01:40:50.535
Have you never seen a Prime Minister
ride on the Underground before?

1419
01:40:53.811 --> 01:40:55.011
What is your name?

1420
01:40:55.111 --> 01:40:56.761
Oliver Wilson, sir.

1421
01:40:58.331 --> 01:41:00.323
And what do you do Mr. Wilson?

1422
01:41:00.423 --> 01:41:02.071
Brick-layer, sir./  Ah!

1423
01:41:02.171 --> 01:41:06.659
Brick-layer! We shall have
great need of brick-layers soon.

1424
01:41:06.759 --> 01:41:08.759
Business will be looking up!

1425
01:41:13.455 --> 01:41:14.605
Progress!

1426
01:41:20.440 --> 01:41:21.627
How old?

1427
01:41:21.727 --> 01:41:23.027
Five months, sir.

1428
01:41:23.278 --> 01:41:24.378
He looks like you.

1429
01:41:25.563 --> 01:41:28.107
Madam, all babies look like me.

1430
01:41:30.917 --> 01:41:32.333
What is your name?

1431
01:41:32.843 --> 01:41:35.711
Mrs. Jessie Sutton./  Ah, Mrs. Sutton!

1432
01:41:35.811 --> 01:41:36.911
What a pleasure.

1433
01:41:38.333 --> 01:41:39.233
Abigail.

1434
01:41:39.932 --> 01:41:41.182
Abigail Walker.

1435
01:41:41.386 --> 01:41:42.578
Marcus Peters.

1436
01:41:42.678 --> 01:41:44.145
"Marcus Peters."

1437
01:41:47.013 --> 01:41:48.763
Agnes Dillon./  "Agnes."

1438
01:41:49.786 --> 01:41:51.726
Maurice Baker./  "Mr. Baker."

1439
01:41:51.846 --> 01:41:54.245
Alice Simpson./  "Alice Simpson."

1440
01:41:54.345 --> 01:41:56.100
Miss Margaret Jerome.

1441
01:41:56.200 --> 01:41:57.938
Ah, Jerome!

1442
01:41:58.830 --> 01:42:03.233
My mother was a Jerome!
I expect we are closely related.

1443
01:42:04.402 --> 01:42:07.402
Please, please, sit.
Sit everyone.

1444
01:42:11.326 --> 01:42:13.082
Thank you, Mr. Baker.

1445
01:42:13.777 --> 01:42:15.370
So how are you all...

1446
01:42:16.096 --> 01:42:17.890
How are you all bearing up?

1447
01:42:19.202 --> 01:42:20.963
Good... good spirits?

1448
01:42:21.564 --> 01:42:22.314
Yes.

1449
01:42:22.788 --> 01:42:23.888
Just as well.

1450
01:42:24.562 --> 01:42:25.912
We still need them.

1451
01:42:28.289 --> 01:42:30.539
Let me ask you all something,

1452
01:42:30.702 --> 01:42:34.223
this thing, a weighing on my mind.

1453
01:42:34.343 --> 01:42:36.793
Perhaps you can provide me
with an answer.

1454
01:42:37.689 --> 01:42:38.489
You...

1455
01:42:39.744 --> 01:42:41.587
The British people.

1456
01:42:42.768 --> 01:42:44.375
What is your mood?

1457
01:42:45.253 --> 01:42:46.153
Is it...

1458
01:42:47.103 --> 01:42:48.401
Is it confident?

1459
01:42:48.501 --> 01:42:50.729
Yes./  Confident. How confident?

1460
01:42:50.829 --> 01:42:51.677
Very.

1461
01:42:51.777 --> 01:42:53.298
Some people say it’s a lost cause.

1462
01:42:53.398 --> 01:42:56.330
Lost cause is the only ones
worth fighting for.

1463
01:42:56.468 --> 01:42:57.481
Too late./  Yes...

1464
01:42:57.581 --> 01:43:00.900
Now let me ask you this.
If the worst came to pass,

1465
01:43:01.658 --> 01:43:07.122
and... and the enemy were
to appear on those streets above,

1466
01:43:07.529 --> 01:43:09.245
what would you do?/  Fight!

1467
01:43:09.345 --> 01:43:12.222
Fight the fascists!/  Fight them with
anything we can lay our hands on!

1468
01:43:12.322 --> 01:43:14.661
Broom handles if we must!/
Street by street!

1469
01:43:14.761 --> 01:43:16.761
They'll never take Picadilly!

1470
01:43:17.684 --> 01:43:19.924
Never take Picadilly indeed!

1471
01:43:20.489 --> 01:43:22.732
And what if I put it to you all

1472
01:43:23.256 --> 01:43:24.431
that we might,

1473
01:43:24.752 --> 01:43:28.450
if we ask nicely

1474
01:43:28.965 --> 01:43:32.839
get very favourable
terms from Mr. Hitler,

1475
01:43:32.939 --> 01:43:36.744
if we enter into a peace deal
with him right now?

1476
01:43:37.399 --> 01:43:39.181
What would you say to that?

1477
01:43:39.301 --> 01:43:40.520
Never!/  Never!

1478
01:43:40.640 --> 01:43:41.747
Never!/  Never!

1479
01:43:41.847 --> 01:43:42.997
Never!/  Never!

1480
01:43:51.210 --> 01:43:53.065
So... never.

1481
01:43:54.943 --> 01:43:55.773
Never.

1482
01:43:57.741 --> 01:43:59.360
Will you never give up?

1483
01:43:59.566 --> 01:44:00.902
No, never!

1484
01:44:02.770 --> 01:44:06.674
"Then out spake brave Horatius."

1485
01:44:09.591 --> 01:44:11.819
"The Captain of the gate:

1486
01:44:13.279 --> 01:44:17.043
To every man upon this earth,
Death...

1487
01:44:19.389 --> 01:44:21.128
...cometh soon or late."

1488
01:44:22.148 --> 01:44:26.514
"And how can man die better

1489
01:44:27.085 --> 01:44:29.782
than facing fearful odds?"

1490
01:44:31.788 --> 01:44:34.805
"For the ashes.../
"For the ashes of his fathers,

1491
01:44:34.925 --> 01:44:37.083
and the temples of his gods."

1492
01:44:47.336 --> 01:44:48.686
Are you crying?

1493
01:44:48.993 --> 01:44:49.993
Crying?

1494
01:44:51.077 --> 01:44:52.777
Yes, yes, crying.

1495
01:44:54.032 --> 01:44:55.582
I blub a lot, you know?

1496
01:44:57.323 --> 01:44:59.673
I think you'll have to get used to it.

1497
01:45:02.137 --> 01:45:04.112
What... What stop is this?

1498
01:45:04.781 --> 01:45:06.331
Westminster, sir.

1499
01:45:07.612 --> 01:45:10.306
Westminster?
It's my stop.

1500
01:45:42.587 --> 01:45:43.837
Prime Minister!

1501
01:45:46.756 --> 01:45:47.656
Sir!

1502
01:45:49.601 --> 01:45:51.043
The War Cabinet...

1503
01:45:51.143 --> 01:45:52.993
Sir! They're waiting.

1504
01:45:53.237 --> 01:45:55.910
I'm due to address the Outer Cabinet.

1505
01:45:56.010 --> 01:45:57.510
What?/  Now!

1506
01:45:57.864 --> 01:46:01.114
I haven’t spoken to them since
the formation of the new government.

1507
01:46:05.392 --> 01:46:07.849
Prime Minister./  Good day, gentlemen.

1508
01:46:09.301 --> 01:46:13.210
I'm about to speak to
my Outer Cabinet in my rooms

1509
01:46:13.451 --> 01:46:17.795
and I extend the invitation to any
of you who would care to join them.

1510
01:46:21.075 --> 01:46:21.975
Let's go.

1511
01:46:40.850 --> 01:46:46.772
This afternoon, I will address the House

1512
01:46:48.575 --> 01:46:51.925
on the matter of our nation’s security.

1513
01:46:53.145 --> 01:46:54.945
At this very moment,

1514
01:46:55.072 --> 01:47:00.415
the War Cabinet is drafting papers
that lay out our willingness

1515
01:47:00.515 --> 01:47:06.807
to enter into peace talks
with Herr Hitler, via his...

1516
01:47:07.209 --> 01:47:09.460
His lacky, Mussolini.

1517
01:47:11.693 --> 01:47:14.640
I have thought very carefully

1518
01:47:15.199 --> 01:47:19.159
in these last days,
whether was part of my duty,

1519
01:47:20.100 --> 01:47:23.815
to consider entering into
negotiations with...

1520
01:47:28.250 --> 01:47:29.500
With that man.

1521
01:47:32.935 --> 01:47:34.185
Then I...

1522
01:47:36.656 --> 01:47:38.156
I spoke with them.

1523
01:47:43.492 --> 01:47:44.692
Oliver Wilson.

1524
01:47:46.428 --> 01:47:48.375
Mrs. Jessie Sutton.

1525
01:47:49.431 --> 01:47:52.319
Mrs. Abigail Walker.

1526
01:47:53.397 --> 01:47:57.362
Marcus Peters and Maurice Baker.

1527
01:47:57.688 --> 01:48:01.881
Alice Simpson and Miss Margaret Jerome.

1528
01:48:02.905 --> 01:48:05.766
Brave, good, true citizens
of this kingdom, and they argued

1529
01:48:05.866 --> 01:48:09.024
strongly, that it was idle to think

1530
01:48:09.144 --> 01:48:11.415
if we tried to make peace now,

1531
01:48:12.047 --> 01:48:15.647
we should get better terms
than if we fought it out.

1532
01:48:16.856 --> 01:48:17.839
The Germans,

1533
01:48:18.434 --> 01:48:21.173
Mr. Baker felt,

1534
01:48:21.986 --> 01:48:23.186
would demand

1535
01:48:23.739 --> 01:48:27.261
in the name of disarmament
our naval bases, and much else.

1536
01:48:27.361 --> 01:48:28.759
And I think he's right.

1537
01:48:29.445 --> 01:48:33.277
Jessie Sutton, speaking for many,

1538
01:48:34.175 --> 01:48:36.651
believes we would then become

1539
01:48:37.128 --> 01:48:39.209
a slave state,

1540
01:48:39.532 --> 01:48:43.599
though a British Government
which would be Hitler's puppet.

1541
01:48:43.719 --> 01:48:46.439
A government set up under...

1542
01:48:46.559 --> 01:48:49.597
Under Mosley or some such person./
No, no!

1543
01:48:49.697 --> 01:48:53.421
And I join with them
in asking a further question,

1544
01:48:53.521 --> 01:48:56.062
a question I now put to you:

1545
01:48:57.372 --> 01:49:01.228
Where should we be
at the end of all that?

1546
01:49:06.779 --> 01:49:08.660
Perhaps some might benefit.

1547
01:49:08.760 --> 01:49:12.873
And the powerful might be able
to parlay good terms,

1548
01:49:13.299 --> 01:49:15.399
preserved in their country redoubts,

1549
01:49:15.499 --> 01:49:18.294
out of sight of the Swastika

1550
01:49:18.520 --> 01:49:20.721
flying on the... over

1551
01:49:21.196 --> 01:49:23.318
Buckingham Palace./  Never!

1552
01:49:23.438 --> 01:49:24.538
Over Windsor,

1553
01:49:25.306 --> 01:49:27.290
and they draped on these very buildings.

1554
01:49:27.410 --> 01:49:28.610
Never!/  Never!

1555
01:49:28.730 --> 01:49:30.207
Never!/  Never!

1556
01:49:30.327 --> 01:49:31.977
So I come to you...

1557
01:49:33.213 --> 01:49:36.114
I come to you,

1558
01:49:37.204 --> 01:49:39.126
to learn your minds

1559
01:49:39.226 --> 01:49:40.944
in this grave hour.

1560
01:49:42.331 --> 01:49:46.395
You see, it was pointed out to me
by my new friends,

1561
01:49:46.515 --> 01:49:50.988
that you might even rise up
and tear me down

1562
01:49:51.088 --> 01:49:54.477
were I for one moment
to contemplate parley or surrender.

1563
01:49:59.662 --> 01:50:00.774
Were they wrong?

1564
01:50:00.874 --> 01:50:01.896
No!/  No!

1565
01:50:02.112 --> 01:50:03.799
Were they wrong?/  No!

1566
01:50:03.899 --> 01:50:05.023
Were they wrong?

1567
01:50:05.123 --> 01:50:06.866
No!/  Thank you, thank you...

1568
01:50:06.966 --> 01:50:08.453
Then I'm hear you...

1569
01:50:09.149 --> 01:50:10.867
I am hear you!

1570
01:50:13.302 --> 01:50:16.897
It appears to be your will also,

1571
01:50:18.152 --> 01:50:22.434
that if this long island story of ours
is to end at last,

1572
01:50:22.750 --> 01:50:26.335
then let it end only when each one of us

1573
01:50:26.650 --> 01:50:31.080
lies choking in his own blood
upon the ground!

1574
01:50:31.081 --> 01:50:33.285
Yeah!/  Bravo!

1575
01:50:38.209 --> 01:50:40.430
<i>And when I asked to know their minds,</i>

1576
01:50:40.530 --> 01:50:42.945
there occurred a demonstration
which considering

1577
01:50:43.045 --> 01:50:46.482
the character of the gathering,
quite surprised me.

1578
01:50:47.372 --> 01:50:49.322
There is no doubt,

1579
01:50:49.657 --> 01:50:53.536
that if we falter at all
in the leading of the nation,

1580
01:50:53.785 --> 01:50:56.785
we should all be hurled out of office.

1581
01:50:57.146 --> 01:51:00.471
I am sure now that every Minister
on both sides of the house

1582
01:51:00.733 --> 01:51:03.922
is ready to be killed quite soon,
and have all his

1583
01:51:04.022 --> 01:51:07.472
family and possessions destroyed,
rather than give in.

1584
01:51:08.571 --> 01:51:12.452
In this they represent
almost all the people.

1585
01:51:13.048 --> 01:51:17.756
It falls to me in these
coming days and months,

1586
01:51:18.488 --> 01:51:21.419
to express their sentiments.

1587
01:51:24.300 --> 01:51:28.601
There shall be no negotiated peace.

1588
01:51:33.350 --> 01:51:38.289
And you must each do now as you see fit.

1589
01:51:42.753 --> 01:51:44.951
Now if you excuse me, gentlemen.

1590
01:51:46.533 --> 01:51:49.558
I believe I am tend
to address the Parliament.

1591
01:51:50.408 --> 01:51:54.178
And I’m yet to write
a word of my speech.

1592
01:51:54.523 --> 01:51:56.173
There’s the buggers.

1593
01:51:59.217 --> 01:52:00.967
Tiny!/  Sir.

1594
01:52:07.169 --> 01:52:09.033
Miss Layton?/  Sir?

1595
01:52:09.284 --> 01:52:10.684
I’m in need of you.

1596
01:52:13.181 --> 01:52:16.831
We must both now resign.
Force a vote of no confidence.

1597
01:52:17.297 --> 01:52:19.933
Let's go to the Commons first.

1598
01:52:20.631 --> 01:52:22.181
Join our colleagues.

1599
01:52:23.153 --> 01:52:26.553
And speak after the
Prime Minister’s address.

1600
01:52:46.034 --> 01:52:48.291
Here's a woman, who is always tired

1601
01:52:49.282 --> 01:52:51.032
for she lived a life

1602
01:52:51.345 --> 01:52:53.411
where too much was required.

1603
01:53:21.341 --> 01:53:23.053
<i>The Prime Minister!</i>

1604
01:53:32.722 --> 01:53:36.596
Turning once again to
the question of invasion,

1605
01:53:37.292 --> 01:53:40.963
I would observe that there
has never been a period

1606
01:53:41.207 --> 01:53:44.401
in all these long centuries
of which we boast,

1607
01:53:44.843 --> 01:53:50.239
when an absolute guarantee
against invasion

1608
01:53:51.299 --> 01:53:53.699
could have been given to our people.

1609
01:54:00.611 --> 01:54:02.315
I have, myself...

1610
01:54:03.291 --> 01:54:07.741
Full confidence,
that if all do their duty,

1611
01:54:07.861 --> 01:54:10.318
if nothing is neglected,

1612
01:54:10.807 --> 01:54:13.775
and if the best arrangements
are made as they are

1613
01:54:13.875 --> 01:54:15.425
being made,

1614
01:54:15.556 --> 01:54:18.652
we shall prove ourselves once more able

1615
01:54:18.752 --> 01:54:21.102
to defend our island home,

1616
01:54:22.028 --> 01:54:24.933
to ride out the storm of war,

1617
01:54:26.426 --> 01:54:29.300
and to outlive the menace of tyranny.

1618
01:54:29.689 --> 01:54:37.922
If necessary for years,
if necessary alone.

1619
01:54:40.233 --> 01:54:44.271
<i>At any rate, that is what
we are going to try to do.</i>

1620
01:54:45.018 --> 01:54:50.745
<i>That is the resolve of His Majesty's
government, every men of them.</i>

1621
01:54:51.924 --> 01:54:56.757
<i>That is the will of</i>
Parliament and the nation.

1622
01:55:00.100 --> 01:55:03.708
<i>The British Empire, and
the French Republic,</i>

1623
01:55:03.930 --> 01:55:07.754
<i>linked together in their cause
wand in their need,</i>

1624
01:55:08.112 --> 01:55:10.289
<i>will defend to the death</i>

1625
01:55:10.848 --> 01:55:12.600
<i>their native soil,</i>

1626
01:55:14.169 --> 01:55:18.057
aiding each other like good comrades

1627
01:55:18.301 --> 01:55:20.935
to the utmost of their strength.

1628
01:55:21.035 --> 01:55:21.985
Yes!/  Yeah! Yeah!

1629
01:55:24.034 --> 01:55:30.903
Even though large tracts of Europe
and many old and famous States have...

1630
01:55:31.104 --> 01:55:33.854
have fallen or may fall

1631
01:55:34.339 --> 01:55:35.985
into the... the...

1632
01:55:36.085 --> 01:55:38.564
grip of the Gestapo

1633
01:55:39.219 --> 01:55:42.992
and all the odious apparatus
of Nazi rule,

1634
01:55:44.275 --> 01:55:46.784
we shall not flag or fail.

1635
01:55:48.054 --> 01:55:51.195
We shall go on to the end.

1636
01:55:55.953 --> 01:55:58.600
We shall fight in France,

1637
01:56:00.002 --> 01:56:03.676
we shall fight on the seas and oceans,

1638
01:56:04.901 --> 01:56:10.122
we shall fight with growing confidence
and growing strength in the air,

1639
01:56:11.533 --> 01:56:14.947
we shall defend our island,

1640
01:56:15.047 --> 01:56:17.397
whatever the cost may be.

1641
01:56:17.497 --> 01:56:19.671
Yeah! Yeah!

1642
01:56:20.146 --> 01:56:22.785
We shall fight on the beaches,

1643
01:56:23.511 --> 01:56:26.817
we shall fight on the landing grounds,

1644
01:56:27.483 --> 01:56:30.218
we shall fight in the fields

1645
01:56:30.872 --> 01:56:32.172
and in the streets,

1646
01:56:33.310 --> 01:56:35.829
we shall fight in the hills.

1647
01:56:36.414 --> 01:56:38.446
We shall never surrender,

1648
01:56:48.510 --> 01:56:49.360
And if...

1649
01:56:53.964 --> 01:56:54.894
And if...

1650
01:56:57.173 --> 01:56:59.938
which I do not for a moment believe,

1651
01:57:00.389 --> 01:57:04.765
this island or a large part
of it were...

1652
01:57:05.066 --> 01:57:07.791
were subjugated and starving,

1653
01:57:09.022 --> 01:57:13.209
then our Empire beyond the seas,

1654
01:57:13.649 --> 01:57:16.942
armed and guarded by the British Fleet,

1655
01:57:18.466 --> 01:57:21.767
would carry on the struggle!/  Yes!

1656
01:57:22.121 --> 01:57:24.536
Until in God's good time,

1657
01:57:25.304 --> 01:57:29.820
the New World, with all
its power and might,

1658
01:57:30.597 --> 01:57:35.601
steps forth to the rescue
and the liberation of the old!

1659
01:57:35.721 --> 01:57:37.776
Yes! Yes!

1660
01:57:42.159 --> 01:57:44.962
Bravo, bravo, bravo!

1661
01:58:02.265 --> 01:58:03.458
Hurray!

1662
01:58:04.315 --> 01:58:06.071
Very well!

1663
01:58:07.551 --> 01:58:08.992
Bravo!

1664
01:58:13.925 --> 01:58:14.725
Bravo!

1665
01:58:15.406 --> 01:58:21.056
Victory! Victory! Victory!

1666
01:58:26.476 --> 01:58:27.734
Changed your mind?

1667
01:58:28.638 --> 01:58:32.695
Those who never change their mind
never change anything.

1668
01:58:35.879 --> 01:58:37.179
What just happened?

1669
01:58:38.236 --> 01:58:44.209
He mobilized the English language
and sent it into battle.

1670
01:58:55.210 --> 01:59:02.987
{\move(192,300,192,1)}- Manually Adaption by -
